        'engine_formula' => 'curl_to_json',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Simplify API Integration with the cURL to JSON Converter</h2><p>In the world of "DevOps" and API development, cURL is the standard for testing requests. However, when moving from a terminal to an application, you often need that data in a structured <strong>JSON format</strong>. The cURL to JSON Converter is a professional "developer utility" that parses complex command strings and extracts the method, URL, headers, and payload into a clean, searchable JSON object. This tool is essential for "SEO systems design" when mapping external data feeds or building automated scrapers.</p><h3>The Anatomy of a cURL Command</h3><p>Our tool recognizes all standard cURL flags:<ul><li><strong>-X / --request:</strong> Specifies the HTTP method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE].</li><li><strong>-H / --header:</strong> Extracts key-value pairs for authentication and content types.</li><li><strong>-d / --data:</strong> Parses the selection into a dedicated payload object.</li><li><strong>-u / --user:</strong> Handles basic authentication credentials.</li></ul>By "standardizing" these disparate flags into a single JSON schema, you can easily import your request parameters into Postman, Insomnia, or your own code tests.</p><h3>Why Use a JSON Parser?</h3><p>Manually extracting headers and bodies from a multi-line cURL command is prone to human error, especially with escaped quotes and backslashes. Our dashboard uses robust regex logic to ensure that your API request is <strong>Validated</strong> and ready for production use. This handles the "heavy lifting" of string manipulation, allowing you to focus on logic and integration.</p><h3>FAQs</h3><p><strong>Q: Does this handle multi-line commands?</strong><br>A: Yes, it recognizes the backslash (<code>\\</code>] character used to break lines in terminal commands.</p><p><strong>Q: Is my data shared?</strong><br>A: No, all processing happens client-side in your browser for maximum security.</p>',
