    'entropy-calculator' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'Entropy Calculator - Shannon Information Theory Tool',
      'h1' => 'Entropy Calculator',
      'subtitle' => 'Calculate Shannon entropy for data and probability distributions.',
      'description' => 'Find the average information content and uncertainty of any system or message.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-wave-square',
      'category' => 'algebra',
      'type' => 'interactive',
      'processor' => 'interactive',
      'pro_config' => 
      array (
        'mode' => 'pro',
        'inputs' => 
        array (
          'basic' => 
          array (
            0 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'data_type',
              'label' => 'Input Type',
              'default' => 'string',
              'options' => 
              array (
                'string' => 'Text String (Bits of info]',
                'probabilities' => 'Probabilities (Sum should be 1]',
              ),
            ),
            1 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'input_val',
              'label' => 'Data / Probabilities',
              'default' => 'ToolsHub entropy is awesome',
              'placeholder' => 'Enter text or comma-separated numbers (0.2, 0.5, 0.3]',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'entropy_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>What is Shannon Entropy?</h2><p>In information theory, <strong>Entropy</strong> is a measure of the uncertainty or randomness associated with a data source. Developed by Claude Shannon in 1948, it quantifies the "average information content" of a message. High entropy means a high degree of unpredictability, while low entropy indicates the data is more structured or redundant. Our Entropy Calculator helps you measure this critical scientific metric for both text strings and discrete probability distributions.</p><h3>How Entropy is Calculated</h3><p>The formula for Shannon entropy (H] is: <strong>H(X] = -ÃƒÅ½Ã‚Â£ P(xÃƒÂ¡Ã‚ÂµÃ‚Â¢] logÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â‚¬Å¡(P(xÃƒÂ¡Ã‚ÂµÃ‚Â¢]]</strong>. If you are analyzing a string, the calculator first determines the frequency of each character to find its probability. If you provide a direct probability distribution (like 0.5, 0.25, 0.25], it ensures the sum is 1.0 (or normalizes it] and applies the logarithmic summation. The result is typically measured in "bits per symbol."</p><h3>Applications in Cybersecurity and Data Science</h3><p>Entropy is a vital tool in <strong>Cryptography</strong> for measuring the strength of passwords and encryption keys; higher entropy means it is harder for an attacker to guess the secret. In <strong>Data Compression</strong>, entropy determines the theoretical limit of how much a file can be compressed without losing information (lossless compression]. In <strong>Machine Learning</strong>, "Information Gain" (based on entropy] is used to build decision trees by finding the most informative features in a dataset.</p><h3>Using the entropy Calculator</h3><p>Select your input type: use "Text String" to analyze the randomness of a password or sentence, or "Probabilities" to analyze a mathematical system. The result will show the total bits of information and the average entropy. A perfectly random sequence has the highest possible entropy for its alphabet size, while a repetitive sequence (like "AAAAAA"] will have an entropy of 0. Master the science of information with our professional Entropy Calculator.</p>',
    ),
