        'engine_formula' => 'factorial_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Understanding the Factorial (n!]</h2><p>In mathematics, the <strong>Factorial</strong> of a non-negative integer n is the product of all positive integers less than or equal to n. It is represented by the symbol <strong>n!</strong>. For example, 5! = 5 ÃƒÆ’Ã¢â‚¬â€ 4 ÃƒÆ’Ã¢â‚¬â€ 3 ÃƒÆ’Ã¢â‚¬â€ 2 ÃƒÆ’Ã¢â‚¬â€ 1 = 120. Factorials grow incredibly fast; while 10! is 3.6 million, 70! is already larger than the number of atoms in the observable universe. Our Factorial Calculator is a high-speed utility designed for statistics and combinatorics.</p><h3>Why factorials Matter in Probability</h3><p>Factorials are the foundation of counting. They tell you the number of ways to <strong>Permute</strong> (arrange] a set of objects. If you have 5 books, there are exactly 5! = 120 ways to arrange them on a shelf. They are also used in calculating <strong>Combinations</strong> (nCr] and <strong>Permutations</strong> (nPr], which are the building blocks of "Probability Theory," "Genetics," and "Game Design." Without factorials, we couldn\'t calculate the odds of winning a lottery or the likelihood of a genetic mutation.</p><h3>Special Cases: 0! and Stirling\'s Approximation</h3><p>One of the most common questions in math is: "Why is 0! equal to 1?" In combinatorics, there is exactly one way to arrange zero objects (the empty set]. This convention makes all higher probability formulas work correctly. For very large factorials that exceed standard calculator limits, mathematicians use <strong>Stirling\'s Approximation</strong>: n! ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Â°Ã‹â€  ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã…Â¡(2ÃƒÂÃ¢â€šÂ¬n](n/e]ÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â¿. Our tool provides the exact result for inputs up to 170 and explains the scientific notation for larger values.</p><h3>Using the Factorial Tool</h3><p>Simply enter your value for n. The calculator will provide the full integer result, the prime factorization of the factorial, and "Trailing Zeros" (how many zeros the number ends with]. It also shows you the "Next Factorial" to help you understand the growth rate. Whether you are studying "Discrete Math" or preparing for a "Data Science" interview, the Factorial Calculator is an essential part of your toolbox. Master the math of arrangements with our professional Factorial Calculator.</p>',
