      'content' => '<h2>Understanding Graph Degree Sequences</h2><p>In "Graph Theory," every node (vertex] in a graph has a "degree," which is the number of edges connected to it. A <strong>Degree Sequence</strong> is simply a list of these numbers for the entire graph. However, not every list of numbers can actually form a "Simple Graph" (a graph with no loops or multiple edges]. For example, you cannot have a graph where one node has a degree of 10 if there are only 3 nodes in total. Our Graph Degree Sequence Validator determines if your sequence is "Graphic" (valid].</p><h3>The Havel-Hakimi Algorithm</h3><p>Our tool uses the famous <strong>Havel-Hakimi Algorithm</strong> to validate your input. The algorithm works through a recursive process of sorting the sequence and "removing" the highest degree node while decrementing its neighbors. If the process ends with all zeros, the sequence is valid. Our validator also checks the "Handshaking Lemma," which states that the sum of degrees must be even (because each edge is counted twice, once for each endpoint].</p><h3>Applications in Network Science</h3><p>Validating degree sequences is a fundamental step in <strong>Network Analysis</strong>. Researchers use it to ensure their computer-generated models of "Social Networks" or "Protein-Protein Interaction Networks" are mathematically possible. It is also used in "Structural Engineering" to verify the connectivity of truss systems. If you are building a graph or studying for a "Computer Science" exam, this tool is the standard way to verify your theory.</p><h3>Using the Validator Tool</h3><p>Enter your list of integers separated by commas. The calculator will immediately tell you "YES" (Graphic] or "NO" (Non-Graphic]. If the sequence is invalid, it explains the reason (e.g., "Odd sum" or "Too many edges for a single node"]. This tool replaces tedious manual calculations and provides instant verification for "Discrete Mathematics" problems. Master graph theory foundations with our professional Graph Degree Sequence Validator.</p>',
