      'content' => '<h2>The Science of Half-Life</h2><p>In "Physics" and "Chemistry," <strong>Half-Life</strong> is the time required for a quantity to reduce to exactly half its initial value. The term is most commonly used in "Nuclear Decay" to describe how long radioactive isotopes remain active, but it also applies to chemical reactions and the elimination of substances from the body. Our Half-Life Calculator is an all-in-one solver that handles all four variables in the decay equation.</p><h3>The Half-Life Formula</h3><p>The relationship is defined by: <strong>NÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã…â€œ = NÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬ * (1/2]ÃƒÂ¡Ã‚ÂµÃ¢â‚¬â€ÃƒÂ¡Ã‚ÂÃ…Â¸ÃƒÅ Ã‚Â°</strong>. 
        - <strong>NÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã…â€œ:</strong> Remaining quantity after time t.
        - <strong>NÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬:</strong> Starting quantity.
        - <strong>t:</strong> Total time elapsed.
