    'minimum-spanning-tree-calculator' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'Minimum Spanning Tree Calculator - Kruskal\'s Algorithm Tool',
      'h1' => 'MST Calculator (Kruskal\'s]',
      'subtitle' => 'Find the most efficient way to connect all nodes in a network.',
      'description' => 'A professional graph algorithm utility to find the MST with the lowest total edge weight.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-project-diagram',
      'category' => 'algebra',
      'type' => 'interactive',
      'processor' => 'interactive',
      'pro_config' => 
      array (
        'mode' => 'pro',
        'inputs' => 
        array (
          'basic' => 
          array (
            0 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'mst_edges',
              'label' => 'Network Edges (Node1-Node2:Weight]',
              'default' => 'A-B:7, A-D:5, B-C:8, B-D:9, B-E:7, C-E:5, D-E:15, D-F:6, E-F:8, E-G:9, F-G:11',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'mst_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Introduction to Minimum Spanning Trees (MST]</h2><p>A <strong>Minimum Spanning Tree</strong> is a subset of the edges of a connected, weighted graph that connects all the vertices together, without any cycles and with the minimum possible total edge weight. In simple terms, it is the "cheapest" way to link all points in a network. Our MST Calculator uses <strong>Kruskal\'s Algorithm</strong> to find this optimal structure, which is a fundamental task in "Operations Research" and "Network Design."</p><h3>How Kruskal\'s Algorithm Works</h3><p>Kruskal\'s is a "Greedy Algorithm" that builds the MST one edge at a time. It works by:
        1. Sorting all edges by their weight from smallest to largest.
        2. Adding the smallest edge to the tree if it doesn\'t create a cycle with the edges already chosen.
        3. Repeating until all nodes are connected.
        Our tool performs this edge sorting and cycle detection (using a "Disjoint Set" data structure] automatically to ensure the most efficient connection path is found.</p><h3>Real-World Infrastructure Applications</h3><p>MST algorithms are used daily in <strong>Telecommunications</strong> to design cable layouts that use the least amount of fiber optic cable to connect multiple cities. In <strong>Civil Engineering</strong>, they help plan "Water Supply Systems" and electrical grids to minimize construction costs. They are even used in "Computer Science" for cluster analysis and "Image Processing" to segment parts of a picture. Any time you need to connect multiple locations with minimum cost, an MST is the answer.</p><h3>Using the MST Solver</h3><p>Provide your network structure using an "Edge List." Enter each connection in the format "A-B:10" (Connection between A and B with cost 10]. The calculator will output the "Total MST Weight" and a list of the specific edges that should be used to build the network. You will also see a step-by-step log of which edges were accepted and which were rejected (to avoid cycles]. Optimize your network designs with our professional Minimum Spanning Tree Calculator.</p>',
    ),
