    'hypotenuse-calculator' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'Hypotenuse Calculator - Right Triangle Solver',
      'h1' => 'Hypotenuse Calculator',
      'subtitle' => 'Calculate the hypotenuse of a right triangle using the Pythagorean theorem.',
      'description' => 'Find the length of the hypotenuse given two legs of a right triangle instantly.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-ruler-combined',
      'category' => 'calculators',
      'processor' => 'interactive',
      'type' => 'interactive',
      'pro_config' => 
      array (
        'mode' => 'pro',
        'inputs' => 
        array (
          'basic' => 
          array (
            0 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'side_a',
              'label' => 'Side A (Leg 1]',
              'default' => 3,
              'min' => 0,
            ),
            1 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'side_b',
              'label' => 'Side B (Leg 2]',
              'default' => 4,
              'min' => 0,
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'hypotenuse_calc',
        'examples' => 
        array (
          0 => 
          array (
            'label' => '3-4-5',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'side_a' => 3,
              'side_b' => 4,
            ),
          ),
          1 => 
          array (
            'label' => '5-12-13',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'side_a' => 5,
              'side_b' => 12,
            ),
          ),
          2 => 
          array (
            'label' => '8-15-17',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'side_a' => 8,
              'side_b' => 15,
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Understanding the Hypotenuse: The Longest Side of a Right Triangle</h2>
<p>The <strong>hypotenuse</strong> is the longest side of a right triangle, always located opposite the 90-degree (right] angle. It is one of the most fundamental concepts in Euclidean geometry, forming the basis for the Pythagorean theorem ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â arguably the most famous equation in all of mathematics. Whether you are a student tackling your first geometry assignment, an architect designing a roof, or an engineer calculating cable lengths, understanding the hypotenuse is essential to your work.</p>
<h3>The Pythagorean Theorem Explained</h3>
<p>The relationship between the hypotenuse and the other two sides (called "legs"] is elegantly described by the Pythagorean theorem: <strong>aÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â² + bÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â² = cÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â²</strong>, where <em>c</em> is the hypotenuse and <em>a</em> and <em>b</em> are the two legs. To find the hypotenuse, you simply take the square root of the sum of the squares of the two legs: <strong>c = ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã…Â¡(aÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â² + bÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â²]</strong>. For example, if leg A = 3 and leg B = 4, then c = ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã…Â¡(9 + 16] = ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã…Â¡25 = 5. This 3-4-5 triple is the most famous Pythagorean triple, a set of three integers that satisfy the theorem perfectly.</p>
<h3>Real-World Applications</h3>
<p>The hypotenuse calculation appears in countless practical scenarios. <strong>Construction workers</strong> use it to ensure walls are perfectly square by measuring the diagonal. <strong>Surveyors</strong> calculate straight-line distances across uneven terrain. <strong>Navigation systems</strong> use it to compute the shortest path between two GPS coordinates. In <strong>computer graphics</strong>, the hypotenuse formula is used millions of times per frame to calculate distances between pixels, determine collision detection, and render 3D scenes. Even in everyday life, if you want to know the diagonal size of your TV screen, you are essentially calculating a hypotenuse.</p>
<h3>Pythagorean Triples</h3>
<p>A <strong>Pythagorean triple</strong> is a set of three positive integers (a, b, c] such that aÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â² + bÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â² = cÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â². Common examples include (3, 4, 5], (5, 12, 13], (8, 15, 17], and (7, 24, 25]. Any multiple of these triples also satisfies the theorem ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â for instance, (6, 8, 10] is simply the (3, 4, 5] triple multiplied by 2. These triples are invaluable in construction and carpentry because they allow for quick verification of right angles without needing a protractor or level.</p>
<h3>Beyond Two Dimensions</h3>
<p>The concept extends naturally to three dimensions. The space diagonal of a rectangular box with sides a, b, and c is calculated as <strong>d = ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã…Â¡(aÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â² + bÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â² + cÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â²]</strong>. This is used extensively in physics for calculating the magnitude of vectors, in 3D modeling for computing distances, and in data science for measuring Euclidean distance between data points in multi-dimensional space. Our calculator handles both 2D and conceptual understanding to give you a comprehensive tool.</p>',
    ),
