      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Point to Plane Distance: A Core 3D Geometry Concept</h2>
<p>The <strong>distance from a point to a plane</strong> is one of the most fundamental calculations in three-dimensional analytic geometry. Given a point P(xÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬, yÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬, zÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬] and a plane defined by the equation <strong>ax + by + cz + d = 0</strong>, the shortest (perpendicular] distance is given by the formula: <strong>D = |axÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬ + byÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬ + czÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬ + d| / ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã…Â¡(aÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â² + bÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â² + cÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â²]</strong>. This formula is derived from the concept of orthogonal projection and represents the absolute minimum distance between the point and any point on the plane.</p>
<h3>Understanding the Plane Equation</h3>
<p>A plane in 3D space can be described by the general equation ax + by + cz + d = 0, where the vector <strong>n = (a, b, c]</strong> is the normal vector to the plane ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â a vector that points perpendicular to the plane\'s surface. The constant d shifts the plane along the normal direction. For example, the plane x = 5 can be written as 1x + 0y + 0z - 5 = 0, and its normal vector is (1, 0, 0], meaning it is perpendicular to the X-axis.</p>
<h3>Applications in Engineering and Science</h3>
<p>This calculation is indispensable in multiple fields. In <strong>computer graphics and game development</strong>, it is used for collision detection ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â determining whether an object has passed through a wall or floor. In <strong>robotics</strong>, it helps plan paths that maintain safe clearance from obstacles. In <strong>machining and manufacturing</strong>, it verifies that a tool tip maintains the correct distance from a reference surface. In <strong>crystallography</strong>, it helps determine the spacing between crystal lattice planes. Even in <strong>aviation</strong>, pilots implicitly use this concept when calculating their altitude above a reference plane.</p>
<h3>Step-by-Step Derivation</h3>
<p>The formula is derived by projecting the vector from any point on the plane to the given point P onto the unit normal vector of the plane. If Q is any point on the plane, then the distance D = |PQ Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â· nÃƒÅ’Ã¢â‚¬Å¡|, where nÃƒÅ’Ã¢â‚¬Å¡ is the unit normal. Since Q satisfies the plane equation, this simplifies to the compact formula above. Our calculator performs this computation instantly and shows you the intermediate values ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â including the normal vector magnitude and the numerator ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â so you can verify the result and build your geometric intuition.</p>',
    ),
