        'engine_formula' => 'slope_intercept_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Slope-Intercept Form: The Most Common Line Equation</h2>
<p>The <strong>slope-intercept form</strong> of a linear equation is <strong>y = mx + b</strong>, where <em>m</em> is the slope and <em>b</em> is the y-intercept (the point where the line crosses the y-axis]. This is the most intuitive and widely used form for representing straight lines because you can immediately read off two key properties: how steep the line is (m] and where it starts on the y-axis (b].</p>
<h3>Finding the Equation</h3>
<p>Given a slope m and a point (xÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â, yÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â] on the line, you can find b by substituting: b = yÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã¢â‚¬â„¢ mxÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â. Given two points, first calculate the slope m = (yÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â‚¬Å¡ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã¢â‚¬â„¢yÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â]/(xÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â‚¬Å¡ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã¢â‚¬â„¢xÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â], then find b. For example, with slope 2 and point (1, 5]: b = 5 ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã¢â‚¬â„¢ 2(1] = 3, so the equation is y = 2x + 3. This means the line rises 2 units for every 1 unit to the right and crosses the y-axis at (0, 3].</p>
<h3>Graphing and Interpretation</h3>
<p>To graph y = mx + b: start at point (0, b] on the y-axis, then use the slope to find more points. A slope of 3/2 means "up 3, right 2" from any point on the line. In <strong>data analysis</strong>, the slope-intercept form represents the best-fit line (linear regression], where m is the rate of change and b is the baseline value. In <strong>economics</strong>, supply and demand curves are often expressed this way, with m representing price sensitivity.</p>
<h3>Beyond Slope-Intercept</h3>
<p>Other line forms include <strong>point-slope form</strong>: y ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã¢â‚¬â„¢ yÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â = m(x ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã¢â‚¬â„¢ xÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â], <strong>standard form</strong>: Ax + By = C, and <strong>parametric form</strong>. Each has advantages: standard form handles vertical lines, point-slope is best when you know a point and slope, and slope-intercept is ideal for graphing and analysis. Our calculator converts between all these forms automatically.</p>',
