      'content' => '<h2>Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors: The DNA of Linear Transformations</h2>
<p>An <strong>eigenvector</strong> of a matrix A is a non-zero vector v such that Av = ÃƒÅ½Ã‚Â»v, where ÃƒÅ½Ã‚Â» is the corresponding <strong>eigenvalue</strong>. In other words, when A acts on an eigenvector, the result is simply a scaled version of that vector ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â the direction stays the same. Eigenvalues are found by solving the <strong>characteristic equation</strong>: det(A ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã¢â‚¬â„¢ ÃƒÅ½Ã‚Â»I] = 0, which produces a polynomial in ÃƒÅ½Ã‚Â».</p>
<h3>Why They Matter</h3>
<p>Eigenvalues and eigenvectors are among the most important concepts in applied mathematics. In <strong>Google\'s PageRank</strong> algorithm, web page rankings are determined by the dominant eigenvector of the web\'s link matrix. In <strong>quantum mechanics</strong>, observable quantities correspond to eigenvalues of Hermitian operators. In <strong>structural engineering</strong>, they determine natural vibration frequencies. In <strong>data science</strong>, Principal Component Analysis (PCA] uses eigenvectors to find the directions of maximum variance in datasets.</p>
<h3>For 2ÃƒÆ’Ã¢â‚¬â€2 Matrices</h3>
<p>For a 2ÃƒÆ’Ã¢â‚¬â€2 matrix [[a,b],[c,d]], the characteristic equation is ÃƒÅ½Ã‚Â»Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â² ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã¢â‚¬â„¢ (a+d]ÃƒÅ½Ã‚Â» + (adÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã¢â‚¬â„¢bc] = 0, which can be solved using the quadratic formula. The eigenvalues may be real and distinct, real and repeated, or complex conjugates. Our calculator handles all three cases and computes the corresponding eigenvectors by solving (A ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã¢â‚¬â„¢ ÃƒÅ½Ã‚Â»I]v = 0 for each eigenvalue.</p>
<h3>Diagonalization</h3>
<p>If a matrix has n linearly independent eigenvectors, it can be <strong>diagonalized</strong>: A = PDPÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â»Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â¹, where D is a diagonal matrix of eigenvalues and P is the matrix of eigenvectors. This makes computing powers of A trivial: AÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â¿ = PDÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â¿PÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â»Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â¹. This is crucial for solving systems of differential equations and computing matrix exponentials in physics and engineering.</p>',
