        'engine_formula' => 'first_n_primes_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Understanding Prime Numbers: The Building Blocks of Mathematics</h2>
<p>A <strong>prime number</strong> is a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself. In many ways, primes are the "atoms" of the number world because every integer greater than 1 can be uniquely represented as a product of primes (The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic]. They have fascinated humans since antiquity, from the sieve of Eratosthenes to modern-day supercomputers searching for "Mersenne primes" with millions of digits.</p>
<h3>How Primes are Distributed</h3>
<p>Primes do not follow a simple arithmetic pattern, but they do follow general statistical laws. The "Prime Number Theorem" states that the density of primes near <em>x</em> is approximately 1/ln(x]. This means that as numbers get larger, primes become less frequent, but they never stop appearing ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â a fact proven by Euclid over 2,000 years ago. Twin primes (primes that differ by 2, like 11 and 13] also appear to continue infinitely, though this remains an unproven conjecture.</p>
<h3>The Foundation of Modern Security</h3>
<p>Beyond pure theory, primes are at the heart of <strong>modern cybersecurity</strong>. The RSA encryption algorithm, which secures credit card transactions and private messages online, relies on the fact that it is extremely easy to multiply two large prime numbers together but extremely difficult for a computer to factor the resulting number back into its original primes. This "computational asymmetry" is the foundation of digital trust in the 21st century.</p>
<h3>Generator Features</h3>
<p>Our generator uses an optimized primality test to list the first <em>n</em> primes instantly (up to 500]. We identify the <strong>n-th prime</strong> in your requested range and provide insights into the sequence. This is an essential tool for students studying number theory, programmers building cryptographic experiments, or curious minds exploring the irregular but beautiful distribution of prime numbers across the number line.</p>',
