      'content' => '<h2>Understanding Geometric Sequences: The Math of Exponential Growth</h2>
<p>A <strong>geometric sequence</strong> (or geometric progression] is a sequence of numbers where each term after the first is found by multiplying the previous one by a fixed, non-zero number called the <strong>common ratio</strong> (<em>r</em>]. Unlike arithmetic sequences that grow linearly, geometric sequences grow (or decay] exponentially. This makes them the perfect mathematical model for population growth, radioactive decay, and compound interest.</p>
<h3>Essential Formulas for Calculation</h3>
<p>To analyze a geometric sequence, two formulas are critical:
1. <strong>The n-th Term (aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€žÂ¢]:</strong> aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€žÂ¢ = aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â ÃƒÆ’Ã¢â‚¬â€ rÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â½ÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â¿ÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â»Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â¹ÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â¾. This allows calculation of any specific term in the growth path.
2. <strong>The Sum of n Terms (SÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€žÂ¢]:</strong> SÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€žÂ¢ = aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â(1 - rÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â¿] / (1 - r]. This calculates the total cumulative value of the sequence.
If |r| < 1, the sequence "converges" toward zero. If |r| > 1, the sequence "diverges" rapidly toward infinity.</p>
<h3>Real-World Examples of Geometric Progressions</h3>
<p>Geometric sequences are everywhere. In <strong>finance</strong>, compound interest is a geometric progression where the ratio is (1 + interest rate]. In <strong>biology</strong>, bacterial growth ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â where one cell splits into two every hour ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â is a geometric sequence with r=2. In <strong>technology</strong>, Moore\'s Law (the doubling of transistors on a chip every 2 years] is a geometric progression. They also appear in <strong>physics</strong> as half-life decay, where the common ratio is 0.5.</p>
<h3>Exponential Decay and Convergent Series</h3>
<p>One of the most profound aspects of geometric sequences is the <strong>infinite series</strong>. If the common ratio is between -1 and 1, you can sum an infinite number of terms and reach a finite total: S_inf = aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â / (1 - r]. This paradox ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â adding an infinite number of things to get a finite number ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â is the basis for many concepts in calculus and is famously illustrated by Zeno\'s paradoxes. Our calculator handles both growth and decay, providing precise values for n-th terms and sums.</p>',
