            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'average_deviation_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Average Deviation: A Simple Measure of Variability</h2>
<p><strong>Average deviation</strong> (also known as Mean Absolute Deviation or MAD] is a measure of how spread out the numbers in a data set are. Specifically, it tells you the average distance between each data point and the mean of the data set. While standard deviation is more common in advanced statistics, average deviation is often preferred in some fields because it is more intuitive and less sensitive to extreme outliers.</p>
<h3>The Calculation Formula</h3>
<p>To find the average deviation:
1. Calculate the <strong>Mean</strong> of the data set.
2. Find the <strong>absolute difference</strong> between each value and that mean: |value - mean|.
3. Calculate the <strong>average</strong> of those absolute differences.
The formula is: <strong>MAD = (ÃƒÅ½Ã‚Â£|x - ÃƒÅ½Ã‚Â¼|] / n</strong>. This result represents the "typical" distance between an individual observation and the group average.</p>
<h3>Average Deviation vs. Standard Deviation</h3>
<p>The main difference lies in how they handle outliers. Standard deviation squares the differences, which gives much more weight to points that are far from the mean. Average deviation treats all distances linearly. Because of this, MAD is considered more "robust" ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â it isn\'t as easily skewed by a single bad data point. It is widely used in <strong>forecasting</strong> (to measure forecast accuracy] and in <strong>economics</strong> to measure income inequality or price volatility.</p>
<h3>Practical Interpretation</h3>
<p>A low average deviation means the data points are clustered closely around the mean, indicating <strong>consistency</strong>. A high average deviation means the data is widely dispersed, indicating <strong>high variability</strong>. For example, if two machines produce bolts with a mean length of 10cm, but Machine A has a MAD of 0.01cm and Machine B has a MAD of 0.1cm, Machine A is much more precise and reliable.</p>',
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What does a MAD of 0 mean?',
          'a' => 'It means all numbers in the data set are exactly the same.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why use absolute values?',
          'a' => 'Because the sum of raw deviations (without absolute value] is always zero.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is MAD the same as variance?',
          'a' => 'No, variance uses squared deviations, whereas MAD uses absolute deviations.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Which is better, MAD or Standard Deviation?',
          'a' => 'MAD is better for data with many outliers; Standard Deviation is better for normally distributed data.',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the relationship between MAD and Standard Deviation?',
          'a' => 'For a normal distribution, MAD is approximately 0.8 times the standard deviation.',
        ),
