          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'cohen_d_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Cohen\'s d: Measuring the Magnitude of Change</h2>
<p><strong>Cohen\'s d</strong> is one of the most common ways to measure <strong>effect size</strong>. While a p-value tells you <em>if</em> there is a statistically significant difference between two groups, Cohen\'s d tells you <em>how big</em> that difference actually is in practical terms. It is a standardized measure, meaning it expresses the difference between means in units of standard deviation. This allows researchers to compare results across different studies and different measurement scales.</p>
<h3>The Calculation Formula</h3>
<p>The formula for Cohen\'s d is: <strong>d = (MÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â - MÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â‚¬Å¡] / SD_pooled</strong>. 
- MÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â and MÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â‚¬Å¡ are the means of the two groups.
- SD_pooled is the "pooled" standard deviation, which is essentially an average of the two groups\' standard deviations.
By dividing the raw difference by the spread of the data, we get a value that represents how many "standard deviations apart" the two groups are.</p>
<h3>How to Interpret Cohen\'s d Values</h3>
<p>Jacob Cohen, who developed the measure, provided general guidelines for interpretation:
- <strong>Small effect (d = 0.2]:</strong> The difference is real but might not be visible to the naked eye.
- <strong>Medium effect (d = 0.5]:</strong> The difference is visible and likely has practical significance.
- <strong>Large effect (d = 0.8+]:</strong> The difference is very large and highly significant in real-world settings.
For example, if a new teaching method improves test scores with a Cohen\'s d of 0.8, it is far superior to another method with a d of 0.2, even if both are statistically significant.</p>
<h3>Why Effect Size Matters More Than P-Values</h3>
<p>With a large enough sample size, even tiny, meaningless differences can become "statistically significant" (p < 0.05]. Effect size prevents this trap by focusing on <strong>practical significance</strong>. In clinical trials, psychology, and education research, report of effect sizes like Cohen\'s d is now often mandatory because it provides a much clearer picture of whether an intervention or treatment is actually worth pursuing in the real world.</p>',
