        'engine_formula' => 'compound_growth_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Compound Growth: The Magic of Reinvestment</h2>
<p><strong>Compound growth</strong> happens when the growth of an amount in one period is added to the total, and then the next period\'s growth is calculated based on that new, larger total. Unlike linear growth (where you add the same amount every time], compound growth is exponential. In finance, this is known as "interest on interest" and is the fundamental principle behind building long-term wealth.</p>
<h3>The Compound Growth Formula</h3>
<p>The standard formula for compound growth is: <strong>A = P(1 + r]ÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â¿</strong>. 
- <strong>A</strong> is the final amount.
- <strong>P</strong> is the principal (initial amount].
- <strong>r</strong> is the growth rate (as a decimal].
- <strong>n</strong> is the number of time periods.
This formula shows that small changes in the growth rate or the time period can lead to massive differences in the final total ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â a phenomenon often called "the power of time."</p>
<h3>Real-World Applications</h3>
<p>Compound growth is the engine of the global economy. <strong>Investors</strong> use it to project the value of their retirement accounts over 30 years. <strong>Biologists</strong> use it to model population growth where a species reproduces at a constant rate. <strong>Businesses</strong> use it to calculate Compounded Annual Growth Rate (CAGR], which smooths out the growth of a company over several years to provide a clear picture of its performance trend.</p>
<h3>The "Rule of 72"</h3>
<p>A handy shortcut for understanding compound growth is the "Rule of 72." If you divide 72 by your annual growth rate, you get the approximate number of years it will take for your money to double. For example, at a 10% growth rate, your money doubles in about 7.2 years. At a 7% rate, it takes about 10 years. Our calculator provides the exact values, but the Rule of 72 is a great way to build mental models of exponential growth.</p>',
