    'five-number-summary-calculator' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'Five Number Summary Calculator - Box Plot Statistics',
      'h1' => 'Five Number Summary Calculator',
      'subtitle' => 'Get the core statistics for any data set.',
      'description' => 'Calculate Min, Q1, Median, Q3, and Max to understand your data distribution instantly.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-hand-holding-hand',
      'category' => 'calculators',
      'processor' => 'interactive',
      'type' => 'interactive',
      'pro_config' => 
      array (
        'mode' => 'pro',
        'inputs' => 
        array (
          'basic' => 
          array (
            0 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'numbers',
              'label' => 'Data Set',
              'default' => '5, 12, 18, 24, 32, 45, 50',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'five_num_summary_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>The Five-Number Summary: A Snapshot of Your Data</h2>
<p>In descriptive statistics, the <strong>five-number summary</strong> consists of five values that provide a comprehensive mathematical picture of a dataset\'s distribution. It helps identify the range, the center, and the "spread" of the data, as well as indicating whether the data is skewed. These five numbers are the foundation for creating <strong>Box and Whisker plots</strong>.</p>
<h3>The Five Key Components</h3>
<p>The summary includes:
1. <strong>Minimum:</strong> The smallest value in the set.
2. <strong>First Quartile (Q1]:</strong> The 25th percentile (the "median" of the lower half].
3. <strong>Median (Q2]:</strong> The 50th percentile (the middle value].
4. <strong>Third Quartile (Q3]:</strong> The 75th percentile (the "median" of the upper half].
5. <strong>Maximum:</strong> The largest value in the set.
Together, these numbers divide your data into four equal groups, each containing 25% of the observations.</p>
<h3>Why Use a Five-Number Summary?</h3>
<p>Unlike just looking at the mean or average, the five-number summary reveals the <strong>shape</strong> of the data. For example, if the median is closer to the minimum than the maximum, the data is likely "positively skewed." It also allows you to calculate the <strong>Interquartile Range (IQR]</strong> ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â the distance between Q1 and Q3 ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â which is the best measure of spread for data with outliers because it focuses on the middle 50% of the population.</p>
<h3>Practical Applications</h3>
<p>Five-number summaries are used across <strong>data science, business analytics, and social sciences</strong>. In <strong>real estate</strong>, they help buyers see the price spread of houses in a neighborhood beyond just the "average" price. In <strong>education</strong>, they show the spread of test scores across a class. Our calculator sorts your data and performs the quartile calculations instantly, following the standard statistical methods for finding percentiles in both even and odd-numbered datasets.</p>',
