      'content' => '<h2>The Gini Coefficient: Measuring the Gap of Inequality</h2>
<p>The <strong>Gini coefficient</strong> (or Gini index] is the most widely used measure of statistical dispersion intended to represent the <strong>inequality</strong> of a distribution. Developed by the Italian statistician Corrado Gini in 1912, it is primarily used in economics to measure income or wealth inequality within a nation or social group. It provides a single number that summarizes how "fairly" resources are distributed.</p>
<h3>Interpreting the Gini Score</h3>
<p>The Gini coefficient ranges from <strong>0 to 1</strong> (or 0 to 100%]:
- <strong>0 (0%]:</strong> Represents "Perfect Equality" where everyone has the exact same income.
- <strong>1 (100%]:</strong> Represents "Perfect Inequality" where one person has all the income and everyone else has zero.
In the real world, most countries fall between 0.25 (highly equal, like Nordic countries] and 0.60 (highly unequal, like parts of Latin America or Africa]. A rising Gini coefficient indicates that the gap between the rich and the poor is widening.</p>
<h3>The Lorenz Curve Connection</h3>
<p>Mathematically, the Gini coefficient is defined by the <strong>Lorenz Curve</strong>, which plots the cumulative percentage of total income against the cumulative percentage of the population. The Gini coefficient is the area between the "Line of Perfect Equality" (a 45-degree diagonal] and the actual Lorenz Curve, divided by the total area under the diagonal. Our calculator uses the absolute difference method to compute this coefficient from any list of values, providing an instant snapshot of distribution fairness.</p>
<h3>Beyond Economics: Ecology and Science</h3>
<p>While famous in economics, the Gini index is also used in <strong>ecology</strong> to measure biodiversity (species inequality] and in <strong>medicine</strong> to analyze the distribution of health outcomes or services. It is a versatile tool for any situation where you need to know if a resource is being shared equally or concentrated in a few hands. Use our tool to analyze anything from company salary structures to the distribution of points in a sports league.</p>',
