        'engine_formula' => 'iqr_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>The Interquartile Range: Understanding the "Middle" Spread</h2>
<p>In descriptive statistics, the <strong>Interquartile Range (IQR]</strong> is a measure of statistical dispersion, equal to the difference between the 75th and 25th percentiles. In simpler terms, it is the distance between the "top" of the bottom 25% and the "bottom" of the top 25%. This effectively measures the spread of the <strong>middle 50%</strong> of your data, providing a stable view of variability that is not ruined by extreme outliers.</p>
<h3>The Calculation Process</h3>
<p>To find the IQR:
1. Sort your data in ascending order.
2. Find the <strong>First Quartile (Q1]</strong>: the median of the lower half.
3. Find the <strong>Third Quartile (Q3]</strong>: the median of the upper half.
4. Calculate <strong>IQR = Q3 - Q1</strong>.
For example, if you have a dataset where Q1 is 10 and Q3 is 25, the IQR is 15. This tells you that half of your data points fall within a range of 15 units.</p>
<h3>IQR vs. Standard Deviation</h3>
<p>The primary advantage of IQR is its <strong>robustness</strong>. Consider a salary survey where most people earn $50k-$70k, but one billionaire is included. The standard deviation would explode, suggesting massive variability. The IQR, however, would remain largely unchanged because it ignores the extreme ends of the spectrum. This makes IQR the preferred measure of spread for <strong>skewed distributions</strong> or data containing errors and wild outliers.</p>
<h3>Detecting Outliers: The "1.5 ÃƒÆ’Ã¢â‚¬â€ IQR" Rule</h3>
<p>The IQR is also the standard tool for mathematically identifying outliers. According to Tukey\'s method, any data point that is more than <strong>1.5 times the IQR</strong> below Q1 or above Q3 is considered a statistical outlier. This "fence" helps researchers objectively decide which data points should be investigated or removed from an analysis. Our calculator provides the IQR, Q1, and Q3 values instantly, helping you map out your data with professional precision.</p>',
