        'engine_formula' => 'mean_median_mode_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Understanding Central Tendency: Mean, Median, and Mode</h2>
<p>In statistics, a measure of <strong>central tendency</strong> is a single value that attempts to describe a set of data by identifying the central position within that dataset. The three most common measures are the <strong>mean</strong>, <strong>median</strong>, and <strong>mode</strong>. While they all aim to find the "center," they do so in different ways and are useful in different contexts. A complete statistical analysis almost always considers all three together.</p>
<h3>The Roles of the Three Averages</h3>
<p>1. <strong>Mean (Arithmetic Average]:</strong> The sum of values divided by the count. It is highly precise but sensitive to outliers. Use it for data that is symmetrical and consistent.
2. <strong>Median (Middle Value]:</strong> The number in the middle of a sorted list. It is "robust," meaning it ignores extreme outliers. Use it for skewed data like housing prices or salaries.
3. <strong>Mode (Most Frequent]:</strong> The value that appears most often. It is the only measure that can be used for non-numerical data (like favorite color]. Use it to find the most "popular" or "common" item.</p>
<h3>When One Outperforms the Others</h3>
<p>Imagine a small company with 5 employees earning $30k, $30k, $35k, $40k, and $200k (the CEO]. 
- The <strong>mean</strong> is $67k (misleadingly high due to the CEO].
- The <strong>median</strong> is $35k (a very realistic "typical" salary].
- The <strong>mode</strong> is $30k (the most common starting salary].
By looking at all three, you get a much fuller picture of the company\'s pay structure than any single number could provide.</p>
<h3>Symmetry and Skewness</h3>
<p>The relationship between these three numbers tells you about the <strong>shape</strong> of your data distribution. In a perfectly symmetrical "Normal Distribution" (Bell Curve], the mean, median, and mode are all exactly the same. If the mean is significantly higher than the median, the distribution is "right-skewed" (pulled by high outliers]. If the mean is lower, it is "left-skewed." Our calculator provides all three values instantly, allowing you to perform a quick but deep audit of your data.</p>',
