              'label' => 'Group 3 Data (Optional]',
              'default' => '30, 32, 35, 34',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'anova_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>One-Way ANOVA: Comparing Multiple Groups</h2>
<p><strong>Analysis of Variance (ANOVA]</strong> is a statistical method used to test whether the means of three or more groups are significantly different from each other. While a t-test is used to compare two groups, ANOVA is the go-to tool when you have three or more categories. It helps researchers understand if a factor (like a type of medication or a teaching style] has a measurable effect across different populations.</p>
<h3>How ANOVA Works</h3>
<p>ANOVA works by splitting the total "variance" (spread] in your data into two parts:
1. <strong>Between-Group Variance:</strong> How much the group means differ from the overall "grand mean."
2. <strong>Within-Group Variance:</strong> How much individual data points differ from their own group mean (often called "noise" or "error"].
The <strong>F-statistic</strong> is the ratio of these two variances. A high F-score suggests that the differences between groups are much larger than the random variations within the groups, indicating a significant result.</p>
<h3>Assumptions of ANOVA</h3>
<p>For ANOVA results to be valid, your data should generally meet these criteria:
- <strong>Independence:</strong> Observations in each group are independent of each other.
- <strong>Normality:</strong> The data in each group should be approximately normally distributed.
- <strong>Homogeneity of Variance:</strong> The groups should have roughly the same amount of spread (variance].
If these assumptions are heavily violated, you might consider a non-parametric alternative like the Kruskal-Wallis test.</p>
<h3>Real-World Example</h3>
<p>Imagine an agricultural scientist testing three different fertilizers on corn growth. Group A gets Fertilizer 1, Group B gets Fertilizer 2, and Group C gets Fertilizer 3. After a month, the scientist measures the height of all plants. ANOVA will reveal if at least one fertilizer performs significantly differently from the others, saving the scientist from having to run multiple individual t-tests, which would increase the risk of a false positive (Type I error].</p>',
