              'default' => '33, 33, 34',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'chi_square_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Chi-Square Test: Comparing Reality to Theory</h2>
<p>The <strong>Chi-Square (ÃƒÂÃ¢â‚¬Â¡Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â²] test</strong> is a powerful statistical tool used to determine if there is a significant difference between the <strong>observed</strong> frequencies of categorical data and the <strong>expected</strong> frequencies. It is most commonly used for "Goodness of Fit" tests (does my data match a specific distribution?] and "Independence" tests (are two categorical variables related?].</p>
<h3>The ÃƒÂÃ¢â‚¬Â¡Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â² Formula</h3>
<p>The formula for Chi-Square is: <strong>ÃƒÂÃ¢â‚¬Â¡Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â² = ÃƒÅ½Ã‚Â£ [ (O - E]Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â² / E ]</strong>, where:
- <strong>O</strong> is the Observed frequency (what you actually counted].
- <strong>E</strong> is the Expected frequency (what you expected based on a theory or previous average].
By squaring the differences, we ensure that negative and positive deviations don\'t cancel each other out. The final sum tells you how far "off" your data is from the predicted model.</p>
<h3>Applications in Biology and Marketing</h3>
<p>Chi-Square is indispensable in <strong>Genetics</strong>. If Mendel expects a 3:1 ratio of purple to white flowers but observes a slightly different count, he uses the Chi-Square test to see if the deviation is just random luck or if his 3:1 theory is wrong. In <strong>Marketing</strong>, it is used to test if customers prefer one of four package designs equally. If the observed preferences deviate significantly from an equal spread (25% each], the marketer knows one design is actually superior.</p>
<h3>Interpretation of Results</h3>
<p>A low Chi-Square value means the observed data fits the expected data very well. A high Chi-Square value suggests the "fit" is poor, and the differences are likely not due to chance. To get a p-value, researchers compare the ÃƒÂÃ¢â‚¬Â¡Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â² score against a Chi-Square distribution table using <strong>Degrees of Freedom</strong> (usually the number of categories minus one].</p>',
