        'engine_formula' => 'conf_interval_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Confidence Intervals: Measuring Precision</h2>
<p>In statistics, we rarely know the "true" mean of an entire population. Instead, we take a sample and calculate its mean. A <strong>Confidence Interval (CI]</strong> provides a range of values that is likely to contain the true population mean with a specific level of certainty. It tells you not just "what is the average," but "how sure am I about this average?"</p>
<h3>The Components of a CI</h3>
<p>To calculate a confidence interval, you need three things:
1. <strong>The Sample Mean:</strong> Your starting point.
2. <strong>The Standard Error:</strong> Calculated as (Standard Deviation / ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã…Â¡n]. This represents how much the sample mean might vary.
3. <strong>The Z-Score (or T-Score]:</strong> Based on your confidence level. For 95% confidence, we use a Z-score of 1.96.
The formula is: <strong>CI = Mean Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â± (Z * Standard Error]</strong>. The "Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â±" part is known as the <strong>Margin of Error</strong>.</p>
<h3>95% vs. 99% Confidence</h3>
<p>As you increase your confidence level (e.g., from 95% to 99%], your confidence interval becomes <strong>wider</strong>. Imagine trying to catch a fish with a net. If you want to be 99% sure you catch it, you need a much bigger net than if you only need to be 90% sure. While a 99% CI is "safer," it provides less precise information about the exact location of the population mean.</p>
<h3>Practical Interpretation</h3>
<p>A "95% Confidence Interval" of [100, 110] means that if you were to repeat the experiment 100 times, 95 of the resulting intervals would contain the true population mean. It is the gold standard for reporting scientific results, as it provides a clear measure of the <strong>reliability</strong> of the study\'s findings.</p>',
