        'engine_formula' => 'conf_interval_prop_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Confidence Intervals for Proportions: The Pollster\'s Tool</h2>
<p>While standard confidence intervals are used for averages (like the mean weight of apples], the <strong>Proportion Confidence Interval</strong> is used for percentages (like the percentage of people who prefer apples over oranges]. This is the exact tool used by political pollsters to generate the famous "Margin of Error" seen in election news.</p>
<h3>The Math Behind the Margin</h3>
<p>The formula for the CI of a proportion (p] is: <strong>p Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â± Z * ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã…Â¡[ p(1-p] / n ]</strong>. 
- <strong>p</strong> is the sample proportion (Successes / Total].
- <strong>n</strong> is the sample size.
- <strong>Z</strong> is the confidence coefficient (1.96 for 95% confidence].
The term <strong>ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã…Â¡[ p(1-p] / n ]</strong> represents the standard error of the proportion. It shows that as the sample size (n] increases, the error decreases significantly.</p>
<h3>Real-World Example: Quality Control</h3>
<p>A factory tests 500 lightbulbs and finds that 10 are defective. The sample proportion (p] is 2%. Using our calculator, the manager can find the 95% confidence interval for the entire factory\'s defect rate. If the interval is [0.8%, 3.2%], the manager can be confident the defect rate is below the 4% threshold required for safety certification.</p>
<h3>The "Rule of Thumb" for Sample Size</h3>
<p>For this calculation to be accurate, the sample size must be large enough. A common rule is that you need at least 10 "successes" and 10 "failures" in your data. If your sample is too small, the normal distribution approximation fails, and more complex "Exact" methods (like binomial intervals] are required. Our tool uses the standard normal approximation, which is robust for most real-world surveys and experiments.</p>',
