      'content' => '<h2>The Histogram: Finding the Shape of Your Data</h2>
<p>A <strong>histogram</strong> is one of the most essential tools for data visualization. While a bar chart is for categorical data (like "Apples" and "Oranges"], a histogram is for continuous numerical data. It groups data into range-based "bins" or "buckets" to show the distribution and density of the data points. It answers the question: "Where is the bulk of my data gathered?"</p>
<h3>Binning: The Core of the Histogram</h3>
<p>To create a histogram, the range of the data is divided into equal intervals. For example, if your data ranges from 0 to 100, you might have 10 bins: [0-10], [10-20], and so on. Our calculator automatically finds the min, max, and range of your data, then suggests bin counts to reveal patterns like <strong>skewness</strong>, <strong>bimodality</strong> (two peaks], or <strong>normality</strong> (the Bell Curve].</p>
<h3>Why It Beats a Simple List of Numbers</h3>
<p>Looking at a list of 500 test scores is overwhelming. But looking at a histogram of those scores instantly tells you:
- Is the class doing well? (High peak on the right side].
- Are there two distinct groups of students? (Two peaks].
- Are there weird outliers? (Small lonely bars far away from the rest].
This "shape-based" insight is critical for data science, finance, and quality control.</p>
<h3>Interpretation: Skewness and Peaks</h3>
<p>Histograms often reveal the <strong>Normal Distribution</strong>, where most data is in the middle. If the tail stretches to the right, it is "positively skewed" (common in wealth data]. Our calculator provides the raw frequency counts for each bin, allowing you to easily draw your chart or use the counts for further statistical analysis.</p>',
