              'default' => '2.1, 3.9, 6.2, 8.1, 10.1',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'linear_regression_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Linear Regression: Predicting the Future</h2>
<p><strong>Simple Linear Regression</strong> is the fundamental tool for predictive modeling. It takes a set of data points and finds the single straight line that best represents the relationship between an independent variable (X] and a dependent variable (Y]. This line, known as the "Least Squares Regression Line," allows you to estimate what the value of Y would be for any given X.</p>
<h3>The Equation: y = mx + b</h3>
<p>Our calculator produces the standard linear equation:
- <strong>m (Slope]:</strong> The change in Y for every 1-unit increase in X. It shows the "speed" or strength of the relationship.
- <strong>b (Intercept]:</strong> The value of Y when X is zero. It is the starting point on the graph.
For example, if the relationship between "Hours Studied" (X] and "Score" (Y] is <em>y = 5x + 50</em>, it means students start with a 50 and gain 5 points for every hour they study.</p>
<h3>Solving for Errors: Least Squares</h3>
<p>The "Best Fit" is found by minimizing the <strong>Residuals</strong> ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â the vertical distance between the actual data points and the regression line. By squaring these distances and summing them (least squares], we find the mathematically "perfect" center path through the cloud of data points. This is the foundation of <strong>Machine Learning</strong> and <strong>Financial Forecasting</strong>.</p>
<h3>When to Use Linear Regression</h3>
<p>Linear regression is perfect for <strong>Trend Analysis</strong>. Use it to see how your website traffic is growing over time, or how house prices correlate with square footage. Our calculator provides the slope and intercept instantly, along with clear breakdowns of the calculation steps.</p>',
