        'engine_formula' => 'normal_dist_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>The Normal Distribution: The "Bell Curve" of the Universe</h2>
<p>The <strong>Normal Distribution</strong> is the most important concept in statistics because so many natural phenomena follow its shape: heights, IQ scores, measurement errors, and even the size of bubbles in bread. Its signature "Bell Curve" is perfectly symmetrical, with the mean, median, and mode all meeting in the exact center.</p>
<h3>The Z-Score: Standardizing the World</h3>
<p>Because every normal distribution (like weights vs. heights] has different units, we use <strong>Z-scores</strong> to put them on the same scale. The Z-score tells you how many standard deviations a value is away from the mean.
The formula is: <strong>Z = (X - ÃƒÅ½Ã‚Â¼] / ÃƒÂÃ†â€™</strong>. 
For example, if the mean is 100 and the standard deviation is 15, an X-value of 130 has a Z-score of +2.0. This means the person is in the top 2% of the population.</p>
<h3>The Empirical Rule (68-95-99.7]</h3>
<p>One of the most useful properties of the normal distribution is the "Empirical Rule":
- <strong>68%</strong> of data falls within 1 standard deviation.
- <strong>95%</strong> of data falls within 2 standard deviations.
- <strong>99.7%</strong> of data falls within 3 standard deviations.
Our calculator provides the exact probability (area under the curve] for any X value, helping you calculate percentiles and probabilities without needing a printed Z-table.</p>
<h3>Applications in Science and Engineering</h3>
<p>Scientists use normal distributions to determine the probability of an experiment\'s result occurring by chance. Engineers use it in "Six Sigma" quality control to ensure that 99.999% of products are defect-free. Whether you are checking your test percentile or analyzing manufacturing tolerances, our tool provides the precise mathematical "area" for your data.</p>',
