      'custom_faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What if I don\'t know my LMP?',
          'a' => 'If you aren\'t sure of your last period date, an early ultrasound is the best way to determine your gestational age. Use the "Ultrasound" method in our tool once you have your scan results.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why is pregnancy 40 weeks?',
          'a' => 'The 40-week count begins on the first day of your last period, meaning you aren\'t actually pregnant for the first ~2 weeks. Fetal development actually lasts about 38 weeks.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can my due date change?',
          'a' => 'Your doctor might adjust your due date if a first-trimester ultrasound shows the baby is significantly larger or smaller than LMP dates suggest.',
        ),
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>The Clinical Foundation of Pregnancy Dating</h2>
        <p>Gestational age is the primary metric used by obstetricians and midwives to monitor the progress of a pregnancy and ensure that fetal development is aligning with established biological milestones. Measured in weeks and days, gestational age begins counting from the first day of your last menstrual period (LMP], a convention that technically includes the two weeks prior to conception. This specialized clock is the foundation of all prenatal care, as it dictates the timing of critical anatomy scans, glucose screenings, and the assessment of fetal growth velocity. Understanding your exact stage of pregnancy allows you to better prepare for the physiological shifts occurring in your body and ensures that medical interventions occur within their optimal clinical windows.</p>
        <p>There are two primary methods for establishing gestational age: calendar-based dating and sonographic measurement. The LMP Method (NaegeleÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â€žÂ¢s Rule] assumes a standard 28-day cycle with ovulation occurring on day 14. While this is the global starting point, many women have irregular cycles or variable ovulation dates. This is where an Early Ultrasound (First Trimester] becomes the clinical "gold standard." By measuring the Crown-Rump Length (CRL] of the embryo, a sonographer can determine the age with a margin of error of only 3 to 5 days. If a significant discrepancy exists between your LMP and your 12-week scan, your healthcare provider will likely "re-date" your pregnancy to align with the ultrasound, as it provides a direct observation of fetal size rather than an estimate based on a calendar date.</p>
        <p>Accurate dating allows parents to visualize the incredible transformations happening across the three trimesters. In the First Trimester (Weeks 1-12], the focus is on organogenesisÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âthe formation of every major organ system. By the end of this period, the risk of miscarriage drops significantly. The Second Trimester (Weeks 13-26] is often the period of greatest maternal comfort, marked by the "quickening" or first felt movements of the baby. Finally, the Third Trimester (Week 27 to Birth] is a phase of rapid weight gain for the infant and the maturation of the lungs. Knowing your exact week helps you contextualize symptoms like Braxton Hicks contractions or the timing of the Tdap vaccination, ensuring you are always one step ahead in your prenatal journey.</p>
        <p>Beyond the excitement of tracking growth, gestational age is a critical safety variable. Many screening tests, such as the Nuchal Translucency (NT] scan or the Quad Screen, have very narrow "validity windows"ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âoften just a few days wide. If these tests are performed too early or too late based on an incorrect due date, the results can be clinically misleading. Furthermore, accurate dating is essential for managing pregnancies that go past their "Estimated Due Date" (EDD]. By identifying the precise moment you reach 39 or 40 weeks, your clinical team can make informed decisions about induction or continued monitoring, prioritizing the safety of the delivery for both mother and child.</p>',
    ),
