        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'When will I feel the baby move?',
          'a' => 'Most first-time mothers notice movement between Weeks 18 and 22. This date is highlighted in your second-trimester timeline.',
        ),
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Mapping the 40-Week Journey of Pregnancy</h2>
        <p>A pregnancy calendar is more than just a countdown; it is a clinical roadmap that helps expectant parents navigate the complex biological and logistical phases of prenatal development. By establishing a clear timeline based on the first day of your last menstrual period (LMP] or your estimated due date (EDD], you can anticipate the physiological changes occurring in the motherÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â€žÂ¢s body and the developmental milestones achieved by the fetus. This tool simplifies the "midwifery mathematics" used in clinical settings, transforming raw dates into a comprehensive schedule that aligns your self-education and preparations with the actual age of your baby.</p>
        <h3>The Logical Structure of the Clinical Timeline</h3>
        <p>Obstetric care is traditionally divided into three distinct trimesters, each with its own set of biological priorities. The <strong>First Trimester (Weeks 1-12]</strong> is the period of organogenesis, where the foundation for every system in the body is laid. This is also when most early dating scans and chromosomal screenings occur. The <strong>Second Trimester (Weeks 13-26]</strong> is often considered the "golden phase," as early pregnancy symptoms typically subside, and the baby enters a period of rapid growth. Finally, the <strong>Third Trimester (Week 27 to Birth]</strong> focuses on fetal lung maturation and the accumulation of brown fat, necessary for the infant to maintain body temperature after delivery. Mapping these phases allows you to understand the "why" behind your changing symptoms.</p>
        <h3>Strategic Planning for Prenatal Milestones</h3>
        <p>Using a personalized calendar is essential for the logistical management of prenatal care. There are several non-negotiable windows in a 40-week timeline that require clinical attention. Around <strong>Week 12</strong>, many parents opt for Nuchal Translucency screening. Between <strong>Weeks 18 and 22</strong>, the detailed anatomy surveyÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âoften the most important scan of the pregnancyÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âis performed to verify structural development. Between <strong>Weeks 24 and 28</strong>, glucose tolerance tests are scheduled to screen for gestational diabetes. By visualizing these dates in advance, you can coordinate with your healthcare provider and ensure that no critical diagnostic window is missed due to scheduling errors or lack of awareness.</p>
        <h3>Visualizing Growth and Preparing for Delivery</h3>
        <p>As you approach the final weeks of the third trimester, the calendar shifts from tracking growth to tracking readiness. Most clinical providers consider <strong>37 weeks</strong> to be "early term," meaning the babyÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â€žÂ¢s lungs are generally mature enough for life outside the womb. During this home stretch, your calendar helps you track the frequency of prenatal visits, which often increase to weekly appointments. It also provides a psychological anchor as you reach the "due date" milestone. While only a small percentage of babies are born on their exact EDD, having a data-driven timeline ensures that you, your partner, and your medical team are fully synchronised for the arrival of your new family member.</p>',
    ),
