        3 => 
        array (
          'title' => 'Visual Outcome Graphs',
          'description' => 'Generates a bell curve visualization of the most likely results.',
          'icon' => 'fas fa-chart-line',
        ),
      ),
      'custom_faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the most likely sum of 2d6?',
          'a' => 'The most likely sum is 7, with a probability of 16.67% (6 out of 36 combinations]. This is because there are more ways to roll a 7 than any other number.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does this work for D&D dice?',
          'a' => 'Yes, our calculator supports all standard RPG dice, including d4, d8, d12, and d20, as well as custom-sided dice.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is "The Law of Large Numbers"?',
          'a' => 'It states that while short-term results vary, the average of a massive number of rolls will eventually match the theoretical probability calculated here.',
        ),
      ),
      'content' => '<h3>The Foundations of Probabilistic Thought</h3>
        <p>Dice rolls have served as the foundational medium for probability theory for centuries, dating back to the correspondence between Blaise Pascal and Pierre de Fermat in 1654. At its core, dice roll probability is a branch of combinatorial mathematics that explores the number of ways a specific outcome (the "favorable event"] can occur relative to the total number of possible combinations. A single six-sided die offers a simple 1-in-6 chance for any face, but as soon as a second or third die is added, the complexity of the "sample space" grows exponentially. Our Dice Roll Probability Calculator handles this complexity by identifying every possible permutation to give you an exact statistical breakdown of your game-day odds.</p>

        <h4>Mapping the Sum Distribution Curve</h4>
        <p>Understanding "Sum Distribution" is critical for strategic gaming and scientific modeling alike. When rolling multiple dice, the results are not distributed evenly across all possible sums; instead, they follow a predictable triangular or bell-shaped curve. This happens because there are many more ways to achieve a "middle" sum than an extreme one. For instance, in a standard 2d6 roll, there are six ways to achieve a 7, but only one way to achieve a 2 or a 12. This tool maps these distributions precisely, allowing you to see the "hidden mathematics" that governs board games like Settlers of Catan or tabletop RPGs like Dungeons & Dragons.</p>

        <h3>Strategic Decision Making with Cumulative Odds</h3>
        <p>The concept of "At Least" or "At Most" probabilities is often more useful in real-world scenarios than exact sums. In many gaming systems, a player must roll "at least" a certain number to succeed in an action. Our calculator provides these cumulative probabilities by summing the individual probabilities of all qualifying results. This provides a much clearer picture of your success rate, allowing for data-driven decisions during high-stakes sessions. For example, knowing you have a 58% chance of rolling a 7 or higher on 2d6 can significantly alter your strategy compared to simply knowing that 7 is the most likely individual result.</p>

        <h4>Precision Through Recursive Algorithms</h4>
        <p>Technically, our calculator uses recursive algorithms to handle different dice sizes and quantities, ensuring that even complex rolls like 5d10 (five ten-sided dice] are calculated instantly. While some simpler tools only provide approximations, we use the exact integer combinations to ensure that the results are 100% accurate down to the final decimal point. This precision makes the tool suitable not just for gaming enthusiasts, but also for students of statistics and probability who need to verify their manual calculations against a gold-standard engine. By bridging the gap between theoretical math and practical application, we provide a window into the orderly randomness that defines our world.</p>',
    ),
