      'custom_faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a Normal Distribution?',
          'a' => 'Also known as the Bell Curve, it is a distribution where most observations cluster around the central peak and values taper off symmetrically on both sides.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How is this different from a random number?',
          'a' => 'A standard random number has an equal chance of being any value (Uniform]. A Gaussian random number is much more likely to be near the Mean than far from it.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Where is this used?',
          'a' => 'Gaussian units are used in financial modeling, IQ testing, manufacturing quality control, and simulating natural phenomena like height or error rates.',
        ),
      ),
      'content' => '<h3>The Omnipresence of the Bell Curve</h3>
        <p>The Normal Distribution, often referred to as the Gaussian Distribution in honor of the mathematician Carl Friedrich Gauss, is the most important probability distribution in statistics. It describes a vast array of natural and social phenomena, from the height of humans to the variation in manufactured parts or the scores on standardized tests. Unlike a "Uniform" distribution where every number has an equal chance of occurring, a Gaussian distribution is characterized by its signature "Bell Curve" shape. This means that the majority of generated values will cluster closely around the Mean (the average], while extreme values (outliers] become increasingly rare as you move further away in either direction.</p>

        <h4>Box-Muller Logic and Polar Transforms</h4>
        <p>Our Gaussian Distribution Generator utilizes the Box-Muller transform, a powerful mathematical algorithm that converts standard uniform random numbers into independent, normally distributed pairs. This ensures that the resulting dataset adheres strictly to the laws of statistics, making it an essential tool for data scientists, students, and engineers. By allowing you to customize the Mean and Standard Deviation, you can simulate virtually any real-world scenario. For example, if you set the Mean to 100 and the Standard Deviation to 15, you are simulating the distribution of IQ scores in a general population. This capability allows for complex "Monte Carlo" simulations where you need to model uncertainty in a structured way.</p>

        <h3>Defining the Standard Deviation</h3>
        <p>Understanding the "Standard Deviation" is the key to mastering this tool. In a perfect normal distribution, approximately 68% of your generated data points will fall within one standard deviation of the mean. Increasing the standard deviation results in a "flatter" curve with more variety and frequent outliers, while a small standard deviation results in a "tall" and narrow curve where the data points are almost all identical to the mean. This tool provides the flexibility to create either high-stability datasets or highly volatile ones, providing a hands-on way to visualize how statistics define the variability in the world around us.</p>

        <h4>Robust Data Modeling for Science</h4>
        <p>Beyond academic research, this generator is highly valuable for software testing and algorithm development. Many real-world systems do not receive inputs that are perfectly "random"; instead, they receive inputs that follow a predictable central tendency. By testing your code with Gaussian data rather than uniform random data, you can create more robust applications that are better prepared for real-world user behavior. Whether you are generating 10 points for a homework assignment or 1,000 points for a complex financial risk model, our engine provides the precision and reliability required for professional-grade statistical work.</p>',
    ),
