        'engine_formula' => 'cont_fraction_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>The Beauty of Continued Fractions</h2><p>Standard fractions (like 3/4] are just one way to represent numbers. A "Continued Fraction" is a more elegant and powerful representation that expresses a number as a sum of its integer part and the reciprocal of another number, repeating the process. It looks like a "ladder" of fractions nested within each other. Our Continued Fraction Calculator is a specialized utility that takes any decimal or rational number and expands it into this unique notation, revealing the deep structural properties of the number.</p><h3>Why Continued Fractions Matter</h3><p>Continued fractions provide the "Best Rational Approximations" for irrational numbers. For instance, the continued fraction for Pi (ÃƒÂÃ¢â€šÂ¬] produces the famous approximations 22/7 and 355/113. These are known as "Convergents." A continued fraction expansion is finite if and only if the number is rational. If the number is irrational (like the Golden Ratio or ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã…Â¡2], the expansion goes on forever, often in a repeating pattern. This makes them a vital tool in "Number Theory" and "Diophantine Approximation."</p><h3>How to Use the Continued Fraction Calculator</h3><p>Enter your number in the main input box. You can enter a decimal (e.g., 1.414], a fraction (e.g., 7/11], or even a known constant like "e." The calculator will immediately generate the sequence of integers [aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬; aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â, aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â‚¬Å¡, ...] that form the expansion. It also provides a table of "Convergents," showing you the rational fractions that increasingly close in on your target value. You can adjust the "Expansion Depth" to see more or fewer steps of the approximation process.</p><h3>Applications in Computer Science and Astronomy</h3><p>In "Computer Science," continued fractions are used in algorithms for "Factoring Integers" and in high-precision scientific libraries. In "Astronomy," they were historically used to calculate gear ratios for orreries and clocks to track the irregular orbits of planets with extreme precision. They are also used in "Frequency Synthesis" for radio communications. By understanding the continued fraction of a value, you can build systems that approximate complex ratios using simple, integer-based hardware. Our tool makes this advanced mathematical concept accessible for your engineering and research needs.</p><h3>Why Choose Our Continued Fraction Tool?</h3><p>Most calculators don\\ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â€žÂ¢t support continued fractions at all. Our utility is designed to handle both decimals and fractions with high precision, ensuring that the expansion doesn\\ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â€žÂ¢t break down due to rounding errors. The output is clearly formatted in the standard [aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬; aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â, aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â‚¬Å¡, ...] notation, making it perfect for academic research and problem-solving. Explore the hidden patterns of numbers with our professional Continued Fraction Calculator.</p>',
