      'content' => '<h2>Understanding Derangements and the Subfactorial</h2><p>In the world of probability and arrangements, a "Derangement" is a permutation of elements where no element appears in its original position. For example, if three people (A, B, C] leave their hats at a party and then pick one hat at random, a derangement is any outcome where NO ONE gets their own hat back. Mathematically, this is known as the "Subfactorial" and is denoted as <strong>!n</strong>. Our Derangement Calculator is a precision utility designed to solve these specialized combinatorics problems for research and statistics.</p><h3>The Formula for !n</h3><p>The number of derangements for a set of size n is calculated using the formula: <strong>!n = n! * ÃƒÅ½Ã‚Â£ ((-1]^k / k!]</strong> for k from 0 to n. Interestingly, as n increases, the ratio of derangements to total permutations (!n / n!] approaches 1/e, which is approximately 36.7%. This means that in a large random shuffle, there is about a 37% chance that every single item will have moved to a different spot. Our calculator handles the summation logic and provides the exact integer result for any input up to n=20.</p><h3>How to Use the Derangement Calculator</h3><p>Simply enter the number of items (n] you are arranging. The tool will calculate !n and display the result alongside the total number of possible permutations (n!]. You will also see the "Derangement Probability," which tells you the statistical likelihood of a complete shuffle occurring in a random trial. This is an essential data point for anyone studying "Probability Theory" or working on "Information Security" problems involving data shuffling.</p><h3>Real-World Applications of Derangements</h3><p>Derangements are more than just a party trick about hats. In "Computer Science," they are used in the "Analysis of Algorithms" and in the design of "Anonymous Surveys." In "Ecology," they help model the movement of species when their original habitats are disturbed. They also appear in "Experimental Design," where researchers want to ensure that no subject receives the same treatment in successive rounds. If your problem involves a "Complete Displacement" of items, the subfactorial is your primary mathematical operation. Our tool makes these calculations simple and error-free.</p><h3>Why Use Our Derangement Utility?</h3><p>Calculating subfactorials by hand requires complex alternating sums that are difficult to manage, even for small sets. Our Derangement Calculator is built for speed and accuracy, providing a clean, responsive interface that handles the math for you. It includes "Factorial Comparisons" and "Probability Insights" that you won\\ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â€žÂ¢t find on standard scientific calculators. Master the math of the complete shuffle with our professional Derangement Calculator.</p>',
    ),
    'dijkstras-shortest-path-calculator' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'Dijkstra\'s Shortest Path Calculator - Graph Algorithm Tool',
      'h1' => 'Shortest Path Calculator (Dijkstra]',
      'subtitle' => 'Find the most efficient route between nodes in a graph.',
      'description' => 'A powerful algorithm utility to compute the shortest path between a starting node and all other nodes in a weighted graph.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-route',
