            1 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'start_node',
              'label' => 'Start Node',
              'default' => 'A',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'dijkstra_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Introduction to Dijkstra\'s Shortest Path Algorithm</h2><p>In the field of computer science and network theory, finding the most efficient way to get from one point to another is a critical challenge. Dijkstra\'s Algorithm, named after its creator Edsger W. Dijkstra, is the gold-standard solution for finding the shortest path between nodes in a graph with non-negative edge weights. Whether you are navigating a road map or routing data packets across the internet, Dijkstra\'s logic is likely working behind the scenes. Our Shortest Path Calculator is a professional tool that makes this complex graph algorithm accessible and easy to understand.</p><h3>How Dijkstra\'s Algorithm Works</h3><p>The algorithm works by maintaining a set of "visited" nodes and a set of "unvisited" nodes. It starts at your chosen source and continuously calculates the shortest distance to every other node in the graph, updating those distances as it finds better paths. It "Greedily" picks the unvisited node with the lowest distance and explores its neighbors. This continues until every node in the graph has its "Shortest Possible Distance" from the start verified. Our tool implements this logic with a high-performance priority queue mechanism, ensuring accurate results even for dense graphs.</p><h3>How to Use the Shortest Path Calculator</h3><p>To use this tool, you need to define your graph using an "Edge List." Enter each connection between nodes in the format "Start-End:Weight" (e.g., A-B:5]. Separate multiple connections with commas. Then, specify your "Start Node" (the node where your journey begins]. The calculator will parse the graph, verify that all weights are positive, and provide a "Shortest Path Table" showing the distance to every other node and the exact sequence of nodes to take to get there.</p><h3>Real-World Applications of Graph Algorithms</h3><p>Dijkstra\'s is the engine of the "GPS Navigation" systems we use every day, calculating the fastest route through traffic and across roads. In "Logistics and Shipping," it helps companies minimize fuel costs and delivery times. In "Social Network Analysis," it calculates "Degrees of Separation" between users. It is also a fundamental tool in "Game Development," where it is used for "Pathfinding" for artificial intelligence characters. If you are a student of "Algorithms" or a developer building a routing system, our Dijkstra\'s Calculator is an essential resource for verifying your logic and understanding graph traversal.</p><h3>Why Use Our Dijkstra\'s Solver?</h3><p>Tracing Dijkstra\'s algorithm by hand on a piece of paper is a classic classroom exercise, but for any graph larger than a few nodes, it becomes incredibly time-consuming and error-prone. Our specialized solver provides "Step-by-Step Distance Updates," showing you how the algorithm "relaxed" the edges to find the final path. It handles complex graph structures and provides a clear, tabular output of findings. simplify your network analysis and master graph theory with our professional Shortest Path Calculator.</p>',
    ),
    'expand-polynomials' => 
