        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'function_composition_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>What is Function Composition?</h2><p>In mathematics, <strong>Function Composition</strong> is the operation that takes two functions, <em>f</em> and <em>g</em>, and produces a new function where the output of one becomes the input of the other. It is denoted as <strong>(f ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã‹Å“ g](x]</strong>, which is read as "f of g of x." Mathematically, this means you evaluate g(x] first and then put that result into f. Our Function Composition Calculator is designed to handle linear, quadratic, and trigonometric compositions with ease.</p><h3>The Order of Composition</h3><p>It is important to remember that function composition is generally <strong>not commutative</strong>. This means that <strong>(f ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã‹Å“ g](x]</strong> is usually NOT the same as <strong>(g ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã‹Å“ f](x]</strong>. For example, if f(x] = x^2 and g(x] = x + 1, then f(g(x]] = (x + 1]^2, while g(f(x]] = x^2 + 1. Our tool calculates both variations so you can compare the results and understand the "Inner" vs "Outer" relationship of the mathematical chain.</p><h3>Domain and Range Considerations</h3><p>One of the most complex parts of composition is determining the <strong>Domain</strong>. The domain of f(g(x]] consists of only those values of x in the domain of g that produce outputs (g(x]] that are within the domain of f. This is critical in <strong>Calculus</strong> when applying the <strong>Chain Rule</strong> for derivatives. If you are a student or a data scientist, understanding how functions nest inside each other is vital for modeling complex dependencies and computer algorithms.</p><h3>Using the Composition Solver</h3><p>Enter your equations for f(x] and g(x]. The calculator will output the combined symbolic expression, the simplified result, and the value of the composed function at a specific x (if provided]. You will also see a visual "Flow Diagram" showing how the data moves from the input through g(x] and finally through f(x]. It is an essential resource for pre-calculus and advanced algebra. Explore the logic of mathematical nesting with our professional Function Composition Calculator.</p>',
    ),
