      'content' => '<h2>What is an Inverse Function?</h2><p>An <strong>Inverse Function</strong>, denoted as <strong>fÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â»Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â¹(x]</strong>, is a function that "undoes" the operation of the original function. If f(a] = b, then fÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â»Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â¹(b] = a. Visually, the graph of an inverse function is the reflection of the original function across the line <strong>y = x</strong>. Not every function has an inverse; for one to exist, the function must be <strong>one-to-one</strong> (it must pass the horizontal line test]. Our Inverse Function Calculator helps you find these relationships algebraically.</p><h3>Steps to Find an Inverse</h3><p>The standard algebraic method involves four steps:<br>1. Replace f(x] with <strong>y</strong>.<br>2. Swap the roles of <strong>x</strong> and <strong>y</strong> (solve for y in terms of x].<br>3. Isolate the new y variable using algebraic manipulation.<br>4. Replace y with <strong>fÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â»Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â¹(x]</strong>.<br>Our tool automates this process for linear functions, rational expressions, and simple power functions, providing a clear path from input to inverse.</p><h3>Inverse Functions in the Real World</h3><p>Inverses are used everywhere in <strong>Science and Engineering</strong>. For example, "Logarithms" are the inverse of "Exponents," and "Square Roots" (with domain restriction] are the inverse of "Squares." In <strong>Computer Science</strong>, inverses are used in "Cryptography" to decrypt data that has been encrypted by a function. In <strong>Finance</strong>, they are used to calculate the time required to reach a specific investment goal from an interest rate formula. Understanding inverses is essential for working backward from a result to find its cause.</p><h3>Using the Inverse Solver</h3><p>Enter your function into the tool. The calculator will attempt to solve for the inverse and provide the "Inverse Expression" along with the "Domain and Range" of both functions. You will also see a graph showing both f(x] and fÃƒÂ¢Ã‚ÂÃ‚Â»Ãƒâ€šÃ‚Â¹(x] meeting at the line y=x. It is a vital tool for students in algebra and calculus. solve complex mathematical reversals with our professional Inverse Function Calculator.</p>',
