            1 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'divisor_c',
              'label' => 'Constant (c] in (x - c]',
              'default' => 5,
              'placeholder' => 'If dividing by x-5, enter 5',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'synthetic_division_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>The Power of Synthetic Division</h2><p><strong>Synthetic Division</strong> is a shorthand method of polynomial division, specifically when dividing by a linear factor of the form <strong>(x - c]</strong>. While "Long Division" is universal, synthetic division is much faster because it only works with the coefficients and eliminates the need to write out variables at every step. It is a fundamental tool for finding the roots of polynomials and evaluating functions according to the <strong>Remainder Theorem</strong>. Our calculator provides a complete visual grid of the synthetic process.</p><h3>How the Process Works</h3><p>To use synthetic division, you set up a table:<br>1. Write the coefficients of the dividend in a row.<br>2. Place the value "c" (the zero of the divisor] to the left.<br>3. Bring down the first coefficient.<br>4. Multiply the bottom value by "c" and place the result in the next column.<br>5. Add the numbers in the column.<br>6. Repeat until you reach the end. The last number is your <strong>Remainder</strong>, and the others are the coefficients of the resulting polynomial.</p><h3>Applications in Root Finding</h3><p>Synthetic division is the primary method for tests used in the <strong>Rational Root Theorem</strong>. It allows you to quickly check if a number is a root; if the remainder is zero, then (x - c] is a factor. This is essential in <strong>Number Theory</strong> and <strong>Abstract Algebra</strong> for factoring higher-order polynomials that don\'t have a simple formula. It is also used in <strong>Numerical Analysis</strong> for "Horner\'s Method" to evaluate polynomials efficiently in computer code.</p><h3>Using the Synthetic Solver</h3><p>Enter the coefficients of your polynomial (separated by commas] and the value for c. The calculator will provide the "Synthetic Division Table," the "Quotient Polynomial," and the "Remainder." If the remainder is zero, it will also confirm that you have found a root. This tool is a favorite for students who want to verify their manual work and for professionals solving polynomial sequences. accelerate your math with our professional Synthetic Division Calculator.</p>',
    ),
