    'find-minimum-and-maximum' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'Find Minimum and Maximum - Data Set Analyzer',
      'h1' => 'Find Minimum & Maximum',
      'subtitle' => 'Identify the lowest and highest values in any set of numbers.',
      'description' => 'A professional utility to analyze data distributions, finding range and extreme values.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-arrows-alt-v',
      'category' => 'utility',
      'type' => 'interactive',
      'processor' => 'interactive',
      'pro_config' => 
      array (
        'mode' => 'pro',
        'inputs' => 
        array (
          'basic' => 
          array (
            0 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'numbers',
              'label' => 'Numbers (Separated by Commas]',
              'default' => '12, 45, 7, 89, 23, 56',
              'placeholder' => 'Enter data list: 5, 10, -2, 40...',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'find_min_max_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Analyzing Data Extremes</h2><p>In data analysis, identifying the <strong>Minimum</strong> (lowest] and <strong>Maximum</strong> (highest] values is the first step toward understanding a data set\'s distribution. These "Extremes" define the boundaries of your information. Our Find Minimum and Maximum tool allows you to input large sets of numbers and instantly extract these critical statistics, saving you from tedious manual sorting and reducing the risk of human error.</p><h3>What Extremes Tell Us</h3><p>Beyond just two numbers, the min and max provide context for the <strong>Range</strong> (Max - Min]. A large range indicates high "Volatility" or variance in the data, while a small range suggests stability or "Consistency." These metrics are used in <strong>Statistics</strong> to identify outliers and in <strong>Quality Control</strong> to ensure parts stay within specific tolerances. Whether you are tracking temperature changes or stock prices, the extremes are where the most interesting events happen.</p><h3>Practical use cases</h3><p>In <strong>Business</strong>, finding the min and max helps identify seasonal lows and peaks in sales. In <strong>Sports Analytics</strong>, it identifies a player\'s worst and best performances over a season. In <strong>Laboratory Science</strong>, it helps define the "Operating Range" of experiments and equipment. It is a fundamental building block of "Descriptive Statistics" and "Information Science," serving as the basis for more complex models like the "Five-Number Summary."</p><h3>Using the Extrema Tool</h3><p>Enter your list of numbers separated by commas, spaces, or semicolons. The calculator will instantly find the "Min," "Max," and calculate the "Range" and "Average." It also provides a visual "Bar Chart" of your data to help you see where the extremes sit relative to the rest of the values. This tool is perfect for students, researchers, and anyone managing datasets in spreadsheets. Find the limits of your data with our professional Minimum and Maximum Calculator.</p>',
    ),
    'is-it-a-prime-number' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'Is it a Prime Number? - Primality Checker Tool',
      'h1' => 'Is it a Prime Number?',
      'subtitle' => 'Check if any integer is a prime or composite number.',
      'description' => 'A professional number theory utility that identifies prime status and finds factors.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-hashtag',
      'category' => 'utility',
      'type' => 'interactive',
      'processor' => 'interactive',
      'pro_config' => 
      array (
        'mode' => 'pro',
        'inputs' => 
        array (
          'basic' => 
          array (
            0 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'number',
              'label' => 'Enter an Integer',
              'default' => 97,
              'min' => 0,
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'prime_checker_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Understanding Prime Numbers</h2><p>A <strong>Prime Number</strong> is a natural number greater than 1 that cannot be formed by multiplying two smaller natural numbers. In other words, its only divisors are 1 and itself. Numbers that have other factors are called <strong>Composite</strong>. Our Primality Checker is a high-speed utility that uses trial division and modular arithmetic to determine the status of any integer, whether it is a small prime like 7 or a large one like 1,000,003.</p><h3>Why Primes are the "Building Blocks"</h3><p>According to the <strong>Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic</strong>, every positive integer (greater than 1] is either a prime itself or can be uniquely represented as a product of primes. This is why primes are considered the "Atoms" of mathematics. Our tool doesn\'t just tell you if a number is prime; if it is composite, it also identifies the "Smallest Divisor" to help you start your factorization process.</p><h3>Primes in Modern Technology</h3><p>Primes are the foundation of <strong>Cryptography</strong> and internet security. The RSA encryption algorithm, which secures your credit card transactions and private messages, relies on the fact that it is very easy to multiply two large primes together, but extremely difficult for a computer to factor the resulting product back into its original primes. Without prime numbers, <strong>E-commerce</strong> and secure communications would be impossible. Mastering primes is the first step into the world of "Cybersecurity" and "Computational Math."</p><h3>Using the Prime Checker</h3><p>Simply enter a whole number. The calculator will tell you if it is <strong>PRIME</strong> or <strong>COMPOSITE</strong>. For composite numbers, it provides the "Prime Factorization" and for primes, it shows the "Next Prime" in the sequence. It is an essential tool for number theory students and coding enthusiasts. Explore the secrets of the integers with our professional "Is it a Prime Number?" Checker.</p>',
    ),
