        'engine_formula' => 'fourier_series_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>The Art of Harmonic Decomposition</h2><p><strong>Fourier Series</strong> is a mathematical tool used to represent any periodic function as a sum of simple sine and cosine waves. Named after Joseph Fourier, this theorem states that complex waves (like square waves or sawteeth] can be "Decomposed" into their fundamental components. Our Fourier Series Coefficients Calculator finds the essential triplets <strong>aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬</strong>, <strong>aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€žÂ¢</strong>, and <strong>bÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€žÂ¢</strong> that define these components, allowing you to reconstruct complex signals with precision.</p><h3>The Integral Formulas</h3><p>The coefficients are found using Euler\'s formulas over the interval [-L, L]:<br>1. <strong>aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬ = (1/L] ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã‚Â« f(x] dx</strong><br>2. <strong>aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€žÂ¢ = (1/L] ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã‚Â« f(x] cos(nÃƒÂÃ¢â€šÂ¬x/L] dx</strong><br>3. <strong>bÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€žÂ¢ = (1/L] ÃƒÂ¢Ã‹â€ Ã‚Â« f(x] sin(nÃƒÂÃ¢â€šÂ¬x/L] dx</strong><br>Our tool uses numerical integration (Simpson\'s rule] to approximate these integrals for any user-defined function, providing the specific weights for each harmonic frequency in your series.</p><h3>Applications in Communication and Audio</h3><p>In <strong>Telecommunications</strong>, Fourier analysis is used to multiplex signals and transmit data over various frequencies. In <strong>Audio Synthesis</strong>, it is used to create "Timbre" by adding harmonics to a base sine wave. In <strong>Vibration Analysis</strong>, engineers use it to find the resonant frequencies of buildings and bridges during earthquakes. If a phenomenon repeats in time or space, Fourier series is the standard language for describing its internal structure.</p><h3>Using the Fourier Solver</h3><p>Enter your function f(x] and the number of harmonics you wish to calculate. The calculator will provide a table of coefficients for each value of n. You will also see a visual "Frequency Spectrum" (Amplitude vs. n] and a "Reconstructed Wave" plot showing how many harmonics are needed to reach your target shape. This is an essential tool for signal processing and advanced calculus students. Analyze the rhythm of math with our professional Fourier Series Calculator.</p>',
    ),
    'radius-of-convergence' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'Radius of Convergence Calculator - Power Series Tool',
      'h1' => 'Radius of Convergence Calculator',
      'subtitle' => 'Find the interval where a power series converges.',
      'description' => 'A professional utility using the Ratio Test to determine the bounds of a series.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-compress-arrows-alt',
      'category' => 'calculators',
      'type' => 'interactive',
      'processor' => 'interactive',
      'pro_config' => 
      array (
        'mode' => 'pro',
        'inputs' => 
        array (
          'basic' => 
          array (
            0 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'a_n',
              'label' => 'Coefficient Term aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€žÂ¢',
              'default' => '1 / n',
              'placeholder' => 'e.g., 2^n / n!',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'radius_convergence_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>The Limits of Infinite Series</h2><p>In calculus, a <strong>Power Series</strong> is an infinite sum of terms involving powers of (x - c]. The <strong>Radius of Convergence (R]</strong> tells us the maximum distance from the center (c] for which the series produces a finite, meaningful result. Outside of this radius, the series "Diverges" to infinity and becomes meaningless. Our Radius of Convergence Calculator uses the <strong>Ratio Test</strong> to find this critical bound for any given coefficient sequence aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€žÂ¢.</p><h3>Applying the Ratio Test</h3><p>The radius is usually found by taking the limit of the ratio of consecutive terms:<br><strong>L = lim |aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€žÂ¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã…Â ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â / aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€žÂ¢|</strong> as n approaches infinity.<br>The Radius of Convergence is then <strong>R = 1 / L</strong>. If L is 0, the radius is infinite (converges everywhere]. If L is infinite, the radius is 0 (converges only at the center]. our tool performs this limit analysis numerically to help you find the "Convergence Disk" for your series.</p><h3>Importance in Complex Analysis</h3><p>Finding the radius of convergence is a fundamental task in <strong>Complex Analysis</strong> and <strong>Theoretical Physics</strong>. It defines the "Safe Zone" for using Taylor and Maclaurin series to approximate functions like sin(x] or e^x. In <strong>Engineering</strong>, it is used in the "Method of Frobenius" to solve differential equations around regular singular points. Without knowing the radius, an infinite series can lead to "Mathematical Paradoxes" and invalid engineering models.</p><h3>How to Use the Convergence Solver</h3><p>Input your coefficient term aÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€žÂ¢ as a function of n. The calculator will estimate the limit L and provide the "Radius R" and the "Interval of Convergence." You will see a step-by-step breakdown of the Ratio Test calculation. This utility is the gold standard for checking series homework and researching analytic functions. Master the boundaries of infinity with our professional Radius of Convergence Calculator.</p>',
    ),
    'runge-kutta-rk4-method' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'Runge-Kutta (RK4] Method Calculator - ODE Solver',
      'h1' => 'Runge-Kutta (RK4] Calculator',
      'subtitle' => 'Solve first-order differential equations using the classical RK4 algorithm.',
      'description' => 'A professional numerical utility for precise ODE integration and trajectory modeling.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-rocket',
      'category' => 'calculators',
      'type' => 'interactive',
      'processor' => 'interactive',
      'pro_config' => 
      array (
        'mode' => 'pro',
        'inputs' => 
        array (
          'basic' => 
          array (
            0 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'dy_dx',
              'label' => 'dy/dx = f(x, y]',
              'default' => 'x + y',
              'placeholder' => 'e.g., -y + x or 2*x*y',
            ),
            1 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'x0',
              'label' => 'Initial xÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬',
              'default' => 0,
            ),
            2 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'y0',
              'label' => 'Initial yÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã¢â€šÂ¬',
              'default' => 1,
            ),
            3 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'h',
              'label' => 'Step Size (h]',
