        'engine_formula' => 'reduce_fractions_calc',
        'examples' => 
        array (
          0 => 
          array (
            'label' => '24/36',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'numerator' => 24,
              'denominator' => 36,
            ),
          ),
          1 => 
          array (
            'label' => '15/45',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'numerator' => 15,
              'denominator' => 45,
            ),
          ),
          2 => 
          array (
            'label' => '120/500',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'numerator' => 120,
              'denominator' => 500,
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>The Art of Simplifying Fractions</h2><p>Working with large, bulky fractions like 125/500 can be confusing and prone to error. In mathematics, it is standard practice to "Reduce" or "Simplify" fractions to their <strong>Lowest Terms</strong>. This means finding an equivalent fraction where the numerator and denominator are as small as possible. Our Reduce Fractions Calculator automates this process, ensuring your mathematical results are always clean and professional.</p><h3>The Role of the Greatest Common Divisor (GCD]</h3><p>To reduce a fraction, you must find the largest number that divides evenly into both the top and bottom numbers. This is known as the <strong>Greatest Common Divisor (GCD]</strong> or Greatest Common Factor (GCF]. For example, in the fraction 8/12, the GCD is 4. By dividing both 8 and 12 by 4, we get 2/3. Because 2 and 3 share no common factors other than 1, the fraction is now in its simplest form.</p><h3>Why Reducing Fractions is Essential</h3><p>In **Education**, teachers always require answers in simplest form because it demonstrates a full understanding of the numbers involved. In **Programming**, using simplified ratios reduces the risk of floating-point errors and improves data storage efficiency. In **Design**, simplified ratios (like 16:9 or 4:3] are easier to communicate and implement across various screen sizes. Whether you are doing homework or architectural planning, simplified fractions are the key to clarity.</p><h3>How to Use the Simplifier</h3><p>Enter your original numerator and denominator. The tool will calculate the GCD, show the division steps, and provide the final reduced fraction. It also identifies if the input is already in its simplest form. This utility is the perfect companion for anyone studying "Pre-Algebra," "Advanced Arithmetic," or "Number Theory." Simplify your logic today with our professional Reduce Fractions Calculator.</p>',
    ),
    'box-whisker-plot-maker' => 
