            array (
              'x_data' => '1, 5, 10, 2',
              'y_data' => '50, 10, 90, 40',
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Discovering Relationships with Scatter Plots</h2><p>A <strong>Scatter Plot</strong> is a mathematical diagram that uses Cartesian coordinates to display values for two variables from a dataset. It is the primary tool for identifying <strong>Correlation</strong>ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âthe relationship between an independent variable (X] and a dependent variable (Y]. Does increasing X cause Y to go up (Positive Correlation]? Or does Y go down (Negative Correlation]? Or is there no relationship at all? Our Scatter Plot Maker helps you see the "Trend Line" hidden in your data.</p><h3>Identifying Patterns and Trends</h3><p>When you look at a scatter plot, you are looking for a pattern. If the points form a rough line, there is a **Linear Relationship**. If they form a curve, it might be **Exponential** or **Quadratic**. Points that stand far away from the main cluster are **Outliers**, which often indicate measurement errors or rare, interesting phenomena. Scatter plots are the first step in "Regression Analysis," where we attempt to create a mathematical model for prediction.</p><h3>Practical Uses in Research and Finance</h3><p>In **Medicine**, scatter plots compare "Dosage" (X] vs. "Recovery Time" (Y] to find the most effective treatment levels. In **Finance**, they are used to compare the performance of a stock against the overall market (Beta analysis]. In **Meteorology**, they relate "Pressure" to "Wind Speed." Any time you have two sets of numbers and you want to know if they influence each other, a scatter plot is your best diagnostic tool.</p><h3>Using the Scatter Plot generator</h3><p>Enter your X values and Y values into the respective fields. Ensure you have the same number of data points for both. The tool will generate an interactive chart where you can hover over individual points for details. It is an essential resource for "Sociology," "Engineering," and "Machine Learning" foundations. Master the science of relationships with our professional Scatter Plot Maker.</p>',
    ),
    'logarithmic-growth-calculator' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'Logarithmic Growth Calculator - f(x] = a + bÃƒâ€šÃ‚Â·ln(x] Tool',
      'h1' => 'Logarithmic Growth Calculator',
      'subtitle' => 'Model systems that grow rapidly at first and then slow down.',
      'description' => 'Predict future values for systems following a natural log growth trajectory.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-chart-line',
      'category' => 'statistics',
      'type' => 'interactive',
      'processor' => 'interactive',
      'pro_config' => 
      array (
        'mode' => 'pro',
        'inputs' => 
        array (
          'basic' => 
          array (
            0 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'val_a',
              'label' => 'Constant (a]',
              'type' => 'number',
              'default' => 1,
              'placeholder' => 'Intercept',
            ),
            1 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'val_b',
              'label' => 'Growth Factor (b]',
              'type' => 'number',
              'default' => 2,
              'placeholder' => 'Slope',
            ),
            2 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'val_x',
              'label' => 'Input Value (x] > 0',
              'type' => 'number',
              'default' => 10,
              'placeholder' => 'Input x',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'logarithmic_growth_calc',
        'examples' => 
        array (
          0 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'Standard',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'val_a' => 0,
              'val_b' => 1,
              'val_x' => 100,
            ),
          ),
          1 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'Rapid Slope',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'val_a' => 10,
              'val_b' => 5,
              'val_x' => 50,
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Understanding Logarithmic Growth</h2><p>While most people are familiar with "Exponential Growth" (which accelerates over time], <strong>Logarithmic Growth</strong> is its inverse. It describes a process that increases very quickly at the beginning but then the rate of growth slows down significantly over time. It never stops growing, but it takes exponentially more "effort" to achieve the same amount of increase. Our Logarithmic Growth Calculator allows you to model these "Diminishing Returns" systems with scientific precision.</p><h3>The Natural Log Formula</h3><p>The standard equation for this growth is: <strong>f(x] = a + b * ln(x]</strong>. 
            - <strong>a:</strong> The vertical intercept or starting adjustment.
            - <strong>b:</strong> The growth factor (slope].
            - <strong>ln(x]:</strong> The natural logarithm of the input x (base e].
            A key property of this model is that the result increases by the same amount every time the input is *multiplied* by a constant factor. For example, the difference between x=1 and x=10 is the same as the difference between x=10 and x=100.</p><h3>Applications in Nature and Tech</h3><p>In **Psychology**, "Weber\'s Law" states that human perception of stimulus follows a logarithmic scale (e.g., how we hear volume or see light]. In **Computer Science**, logarithmic growth describes the "Time Complexity" of binary search algorithms (O(log n]]. In **Biology**, it models the initial colonization phase of a new habitat. In **Business**, it often models the relationship between "Advertising Spend" and "Market Share," where the first few dollars have much more impact than the last few.</p><h3>How to Model Your Growth</h3><p>Enter your constants and your input value. The tool will calculate the result and provide insights into the growth trajectory. Note that x must always be greater than 0, as the log of zero or negative numbers is undefined. It is an essential resource for "Calculus," "Economic Modeling," and "Acoustics." accurately predict your benchmarks with our professional Logarithmic Growth Calculator.</p>',
    ),
    'mann-whitney-u-test-calculator' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'Mann-Whitney U Test Calculator - Non-Parametric Comparison',
      'h1' => 'Mann-Whitney U Test Calculator',
      'subtitle' => 'Compare two independent groups without assuming normal distribution.',
      'description' => 'Determine if there is a significant difference between two sets of non-parametric data.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-vial',
      'category' => 'statistics',
      'type' => 'interactive',
      'processor' => 'interactive',
      'pro_config' => 
      array (
        'mode' => 'pro',
        'inputs' => 
        array (
          'basic' => 
          array (
            0 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'group_a',
              'label' => 'Sample Group A',
              'type' => 'textarea',
              'default' => '12, 15, 14, 18, 20',
              'placeholder' => '12, 15...',
            ),
            1 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'group_b',
              'label' => 'Sample Group B',
              'type' => 'textarea',
              'default' => '25, 28, 30, 22, 24',
              'placeholder' => '25, 28...',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'mann_whitney_u_calc',
        'examples' => 
        array (
          0 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'Different Means',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'group_a' => '1, 2, 3',
              'group_b' => '10, 11, 12',
            ),
          ),
          1 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'Overlapping',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'group_a' => '5, 10, 15',
              'group_b' => '8, 12, 17',
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>When to Use the Mann-Whitney U Test</h2><p>The <strong>Mann-Whitney U Test</strong> (also known as the Wilcoxon rank-sum test] is a non-parametric statistical test used to determine whether there is a difference between two independent groups of data. Unlike the T-test, it does not require the data to follow a "Normal Distribution." It is based on **Ranking** the data points rather than their raw values, making it highly robust against outliers and skewed data. Our Mann-Whitney U Test Calculator is a professional-grade utility for clinical and social research.</p><h3>How the U Statistic is Calculated</h3><p>The process involves:
            1. Combining all data points from both groups into one list.
            2. Sorting and assigning a "Rank" to each point (1 for smallest, etc.].
            3. Summing the ranks for each group.
            4. Calculating the U statistic based on the rank sums and group sizes.
            The smaller of the two calculated U values is used as the test statistic. By comparing this U value to critical values (or calculating a z-score for large samples], you can determine the "p-value" and significance of the result.</p><h3>Practical Applications in Science</h3><p>In **Medicine**, the U test is used to compare the recovery times of two different patient groups when the distribution isn\'t perfectly bell-shaped. In **Agriculture**, it compares the yields of two different fertilizers. In **E-commerce**, it compares the "Time on Page" for two different website designs (A/B testing] when the data includes users who stay for 1 second and users who stay for 10 minutes. If your data is ordinal or not normally distributed, the Mann-Whitney U test is the industry standard.</p><h3>Performing Your Analysis</h3><p>Enter your two data sets into the text areas. The tool will automatically rank the combined data, find the rank sums, and output the final U statistic. It provides immediate feedback on group size and distribution overlap. It is a vital tool for "Experimental Psychology," "Epidemiology," and "UX Research." perform professional-grade statistical comparisons with our Mann-Whitney U Test Calculator.</p>',
    ),
    'mean-absolute-deviation-calculator' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'Mean Absolute Deviation Calculator - MAD Data Spread Tool',
      'h1' => 'MAD Calculator',
      'subtitle' => 'Calculate the average distance of each data point from the mean.',
      'description' => 'Determine data variability and consistency using the Mean Absolute Deviation metric.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-arrows-alt-h',
      'category' => 'statistics',
      'type' => 'interactive',
      'processor' => 'interactive',
      'pro_config' => 
      array (
        'mode' => 'pro',
        'inputs' => 
        array (
          'basic' => 
          array (
            0 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'numbers',
              'label' => 'Data Set',
              'type' => 'textarea',
              'default' => '10, 15, 12, 18, 25',
              'placeholder' => 'Enter numbers...',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'mean_absolute_deviation_calc',
        'examples' => 
        array (
          0 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'Uniform',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'numbers' => '10, 10, 10, 10',
            ),
          ),
          1 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'Spread',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'numbers' => '1, 5, 10, 50, 100',
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Understanding Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD]</h2><p>In statistics, measuring "Central Tendency" (like Mean or Median] is only half the story. To truly understand a dataset, you need to know how "Spread Out" it is. The <strong>Mean Absolute Deviation (MAD]</strong> is a powerful measure of variability. It tells you the average distance between every single data point and the overall mean of the set. Unlike "Standard Deviation," MAD is easier to calculate and understand conceptually. Our MAD Calculator is a precision-engineered utility for students and researchers.</p><h3>How to Calculate MAD</h3><p>The calculation follows four simple steps:
            1. Find the <strong>Mean</strong> (average] of all values in the set.
            2. Calculate the <strong>Absolute Difference</strong> (distance] between each individual value and that mean.
            3. Find the average of those distances.
            By using "Absolute" values (ignoring positive/negative], we ensure that distances on both sides of the mean are counted equally. The result is a single number that represents the "Consistency" of your data.</p><h3>Why Is MAD Useful?</h3><p>In **Education**, MAD can show the consistency of a student\'s grades; a low MAD means they always perform near their average, while a high MAD means they have extreme highs and lows. In **Investment**, MAD is used as a measure of "Risk" or volatility in asset prices. In **Manufacturing**, it is used in "Process Capability Analysis" to ensure that products are consistently close to the target specification. It is more intuitive than standard deviation because it works in the same units as the original data.</p><h3>Using the MAD Solver</h3><p>Paste your numbers into the textarea, separated by commas or spaces. The tool will calculate the mean, list all individual deviations, and provide the final Mean Absolute Deviation. You will also see insights into what the score means for your data consistency. It is a vital resource for "Introductory Statistics," "Quality Assurance," and "Financial Literacy." Master the science of spread with our professional Mean Absolute Deviation Calculator.</p>',
    ),
    'quartile-deviation-calculator' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'Quartile Deviation Calculator - Semi-Interquartile Range ...',
      'h1' => 'Quartile Deviation Calculator',
      'subtitle' => 'Calculate the spread between the middle 50% of your data.',
      'description' => 'Professional variability tool to find the semi-interquartile range and quartile spread.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-border-style',
      'category' => 'statistics',
      'type' => 'interactive',
      'processor' => 'interactive',
      'pro_config' => 
      array (
        'mode' => 'pro',
        'inputs' => 
        array (
          'basic' => 
          array (
            0 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'numbers',
              'label' => 'Data Set',
              'type' => 'textarea',
              'default' => '12, 15, 18, 20, 22, 25, 30',
              'placeholder' => 'Enter numbers...',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'quartile_deviation_calc',
        'examples' => 
        array (
          0 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'Small Sample',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'numbers' => '1, 5, 10, 15, 20',
            ),
          ),
          1 => 
          array (
            'label' => 'Highly Skewed',
            'values' => 
            array (
              'numbers' => '1, 2, 3, 4, 1000',
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>What is Quartile Deviation?</h2><p>In statistics, <strong>Quartile Deviation</strong> (also known as the Semi-Interquartile Range] is a measure of dispersion. It is half the distance between the Third Quartile (Q3] and the First Quartile (Q1]. While the "Range" uses the absolute min and max of your data, Quartile Deviation only looks at the middle 50%. This makes it a **Robust Measure**, meaning it is not affected by extreme outliers. Our Quartile Deviation Calculator provides high-accuracy results for any numerical dataset.</p><h3>Calculating the Spread</h3><p>The formula for Quartile Deviation is: <strong>QD = (Q3 - Q1] / 2</strong>. 
            - <strong>Q1 (Lower Quartile]:</strong> The value below which 25% of the data falls.
            - <strong>Q3 (Upper Quartile]:</strong> The value below which 75% of the data falls.
            Because it ignores the "tails" of the distribution, it provides a better sense of where the "Typical" data lies. If your dataset has "Dirty " data or extreme anomalies, QD is often the best metric to use.</p><h3>When to Choose QD over Standard Deviation</h3><p>Standard deviation squares all distances from the mean, which heavily penalizes large gaps. This makes it very sensitive to extreme values. In **Salary Surveys**, a few billionaires can skew the standard deviation of an entire region. In contrast, <strong>Quartile Deviation</strong> will focus on the middle earners, giving a much more realistic picture of the local economy. It is also preferred when dealing with "Ordinal" data where the mean might not be mathematically valid.</p><h3>How to Use the calculator</h3><p>Paste your data into the input field. The tool will sort your numbers, identify Q1 and Q3, and output the final Quartile Deviation. You will also see the full "Interquartile Range" (IQR] for comparison. It is an essential tool for "Social Sciences," "Market Research," and "Public Policy Analysis." simplify your data spread analysis with our professional Quartile Deviation Calculator.</p>',
