              'type' => 'textarea',
              'default' => 'SHA224 Sample',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'sha224_hash_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Everything You Need to Know About SHA-224 Hashing</h2><p>The <strong>SHA-224 (Secure Hash Algorithm 224]</strong> is a cryptographic hash function that is part of the SHA-2 family. While often overshadowed by its more famous sibling, SHA-256, SHA-224 is a vital tool for developers requiring a specific bit-length for their data integrity checks. It produces a 224-bit (28-byte] hash value, typically represented as a 56-digit hexadecimal number.</p><h3>The Specialized Truncated Hash</h3><p>SHA-224 is essentially a truncated version of SHA-256. It uses the same mathematical structure and constants as SHA-256 but starts with different initial hash values and discards the final 32 bits of the output. This was intentionally designed by the NSA to provide a security level matching 2-key Triple DES, which was a common requirement for older governmental and financial systems during the transition to modern encryption standards.</p><h3>Security and Collision Resistance</h3><p>From a security perspective, SHA-224 offers a 112-bit security level against collision attacks. While modern systems increasingly favor 128-bit security (found in SHA-256], SHA-224 remains mathematically robust. No practical collision attacks have been discovered against the full SHA-224 algorithm to date. It is considered a secure, "professional-grade" choice for ensuring that message data has not been tampered with during transmission or storage.</p><h3>How SHA-224 Compares to SHA-256</h3><p>The primary difference between the two is the output size. SHA-256 provides a 32-byte hash, while SHA-224 provides 28 bytes. Because they share the same underlying compression function, the calculation speed is nearly identical. Developers often choose SHA-224 when working with database schemas or network protocols that have strict 28-byte limitations but still require the mathematical "fingerprint" reliability and security of the SHA-2 family.</p><h3>Practical Integration and Use Cases</h3><ul><li><strong>Digital Signatures:</strong> Used in specialized certificates to verify the identity of a sender.</li><li><strong>Data De-duplication:</strong> Identifying unique files in storage systems with high efficiency.</li><li><strong>Version Control:</strong> Simulating Git-like object tracking for specific software projects.</li><li><strong>Legacy System Bridge:</strong> Maintaining compatibility with infrastructure designed for 112-bit security standards.</li></ul>',
    ),
    'sha256-hash-generator' => 
    array (
      'title' => 'SHA-256 Hash Generator - Industry Standard Hashing',
      'h1' => 'SHA-256 Hash Generator',
      'subtitle' => 'Generate secure 256-bit SHA-256 hashes.',
      'description' => 'The world standard for data integrity. Generate cryptographic SHA-256 hashes for files, passwords, and blockchain data.',
      'icon' => 'fas fa-lock',
      'category' => 'security',
      'type' => 'interactive',
      'processor' => 'interactive',
      'pro_config' => 
      array (
        'mode' => 'pro',
        'inputs' => 
        array (
          'basic' => 
          array (
            0 => 
            array (
              'id' => 'input_string',
              'label' => 'Enter Data',
              'type' => 'textarea',
              'default' => 'SHA256 Secure Text',
            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'sha256_hash_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Everything You Need to Know About SHA-256 Hashing</h2><p>The <strong>SHA-256 (Secure Hash Algorithm 256]</strong> is widely considered the "gold standard" of cryptographic hashing. Designed by the National Security Agency (NSA] and published as a Federal Information Processing Standard (FIPS], it is the most widely used hash function in the world today. It transforms any input stringÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âwhether it is a simple password or a massive binary fileÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âinto a fixed-size 256-bit (32-byte] digest.</p><h3>The Industry Standard for Integrity</h3><p>SHA-256 is a one-way function, meaning it is mathematically impossible to reverse-engineer the original input from the resulting hash. This "avalanche effect" ensures that even a tiny change in the input (like changing a single comma to a period] results in a completely different hash value. This makes it an essential utility for verifying data integrity and building secure digital infrastructures.</p><h3>How Digital Certificates Rely on SHA-256</h3><p>When you visit a website with "HTTPS," the security of your connection is likely powered by SHA-256. SSL/TLS certificates use this algorithm to sign and verify identities, preventing "man-in-the-middle" attacks. Beyond web security, it is used in email encryption (PGP/GPG] and secure software distribution to ensure that the code you download is exactly what the developer released, without malicious injections.</p><h3>Is Your Data Truly Immutable?</h3><p>The security of SHA-256 lies in its collision resistance. The number of possible hash combinations (2^256] is so astronomically large that it exceeds the number of atoms in the observable universe. Finding two different inputs that produce the same SHA-256 hash is computationally infeasible, even with the most powerful supercomputers currently in existence. This guarantee of uniqueness is what makes data "immutable" in professional systems.</p><h3>Why Blockchain Uses SHA-256</h3><p>SHA-256 is the heartbeat of the <strong>Bitcoin network</strong>. It is used in the Proof of Work (PoW] consensus mechanism to secure the block history. Miners compete to find a hash that meets a specific difficulty target, ensuring that once a block is added, altering it would require redoing the work for all subsequent blocksÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âa feat that secures billions of dollars in assets. Our generator allows developers to simulate this hashing process for educational and testing purposes.</p>',
    ),
    'sha384-hash-generator' => 
