            ),
          ),
        ),
        'engine_formula' => 'sha384_hash_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Everything You Need to Know About SHA-384 Hashing</h2><p>The <strong>SHA-384 (Secure Hash Algorithm 384]</strong> is a heavy-duty cryptographic hash function and a member of the SHA-2 family. It is a "truncated" version of SHA-512, producing a 384-bit (48-byte] message digest. It is specifically designed for environments where a high security margin is required, often specified in government and top-secret communication protocols.</p><h3>The Professional Choice for "Top Secret" Security</h3><p>SHA-384 is part of the <strong>NSA Suite B Cryptography</strong>, which is a collection of algorithms intended to protect classified information. While SHA-224 and SHA-256 provide 112 and 128 bits of security respectively, SHA-384 jumps to a 192-bit security level. This makes it significantly more resistant to future advancements in cryptanalysis and brute-force attacks, providing long-term data anchoring for the most sensitive records.</p><h3>Technical Superiority on 64-bit Systems</h3><p>Unlike SHA-256, which is optimized for 32-bit architectures, SHA-384 is built on a 64-bit platform. On modern 64-bit processors, SHA-384 (and SHA-512] can actually perform faster than SHA-256 while providing more bits of security. This efficiency makes it a "realistic" and high-performance choice for modern server-side applications, log auditing, and secure cloud storage verification.</p><h3>Is SHA-384 Quantum Resistant?</h3><p>While no current classic hash algorithm is perfectly "quantum-proof" in theory, the 192-bit security level provided by SHA-384 offers a much larger security runway against Grover\'s algorithm (a quantum search algorithm]. By increasing the search space to 384 bits, it remains a robust defense against "harvest now, decrypt later" strategies that some believe may be employed against lower-bit standards in the coming decades.</p><h3>Key Advantages and Implementation</h3><ul><li><strong>Enhanced Integrity:</strong> 48 bytes of entropy for identifying data corruption.</li><li><strong>Government Compliance:</strong> Meets rigid standards for FIPS 180-4 and Suite B.</li><li><strong>Signature Strength:</strong> Used in certificates requiring high-order digital signatures.</li><li><strong>Collision Avoidance:</strong> Practically zero probability of two different inputs sharing a hash.</li></ul>',
    ),
