        'engine_formula' => 'sha3_384_hash_calc',
      ),
      'content' => '<h2>Everything You Need to Know About SHA3-384 Hashing</h2><p>The <strong>SHA3-384 (Secure Hash Algorithm 3 - 384 bits]</strong> is a high-entropy cryptographic hash function designed for enterprise-grade security. As the middle-tier member of the SHA-3 family, it produces a 384-bit (48-byte] hash value, providing a massive security margin over the standard 256-bit hash functions. It is favored in environments where top-tier data integrity is the highest priority.</p><h3>Advanced Integrity for High-Entropy Data</h3><p>SHA3-384 is often the "professional" choice for signing large datasets and certificates in secure governmental and military infrastructures. It provides 192 bits of security against collision attacks, matching the strength of AES-192 encryption. This balanced, high-fidelity security profile makes it an essential utility for high-security database auditing and sensitive transaction logging.</p><h3>The Power of the Sponge Construction</h3><p>The security of SHA3-384 is derived from the SHA-3 Sponge Construction. In this model, input bits are "absorbed" into a large state (capacity] before the hash is "squeezed" out. This methodology ensures that every single bit of the input profoundly impacts every bit of the output 48-byte hash. This "avalanche effect" is so aggressive that it effectively eliminates the risk of accidental or malicious data modification going undetected.</p><h3>When to Choose SHA3-384 Over SHA-2</h3><p>Many security professionals specify SHA3-384 instead of SHA-384 (SHA-2] because of its resistance to bit-level vulnerabilities found in older arithmetic-based designs. While SHA-2 is still considered highly secure, SHA-3-384 offers a modern, performance-optimized alternative that is better suited for specialized hardware implementations and future-resistant cryptographic architectures.</p><h3>NIST Compliance and Global Standards</h3><ul><li><strong>FIPS 202 Standardized:</strong> Fully compliant with the latest NIST FIPS requirements.</li><li><strong>Length Extension Resistant:</strong> Inherently proof against "prefix" attacks by design.</li><li><strong>Sponge Capacity:</strong> Large internal capacity (768 bits] for high-order security.</li><li><strong>Modern Signature Support:</strong> Compatible with latest digital signature standards (DSA, RSA].</li></ul>',
